\title{Sharpening Kubernetes Audit Logs \\ with Context Awareness}
\author[1,2]{Matteo Franzil\corref{cor1}}
\ead{matteo.franzil@unitn.it}
\author[2]{Valentino Armani}
\ead{varmani@fbk.eu}
\author[2]{Luis Augusto Dias Knob}
\ead{l.diasknob@fbk.eu}
\author[1,2]{Domenico Siracusa}
\ead{domenico.siracusa@unitn.it}

\cortext[cor1]{Corresponding author}
\affiliation[1]{organization={Department of Information Engineering and Computer Science, University of Trento},
  addressline={Via Sommarive 9},
  city=Trento,
  postcode={38123},
  country=Italy}
\affiliation[2]{organization={Center for Cybersecurity, Fondazione Bruno Kessler},
  addressline={Via Sommarive 18},
  city=Trento,
  postcode={38123},
  country=Italy}


\begin{abstract}
    \acf{k8s} has emerged as the de facto orchestrator of microservices, providing scalability and extensibility to a highly dynamic environment. It builds an intricate and deeply connected system that requires extensive monitoring capabilities to be properly managed. To this account, \ac{k8s} natively offers audit logs, a powerful feature for tracking \ac{api} interactions in the cluster. Audit logs provide a detailed and chronological record of all activities in the system. Unfortunately, \ac{k8s} auditing suffers from several practical limitations: it generates large volumes of data continuously, as all components within the cluster interact and respond to user actions. Moreover, each action can trigger a cascade of secondary events dispersed across the log, with little to no explicit linkage, making it difficult to reconstruct the context behind user-initiated operations.

    In this paper, we introduce \ken, a novel approach for streamlining \ac{k8s} audit logs by reconstructing \textit{contexts}, i.e., grouping actions performed by actors on the cluster with the subsequent events these actions cause. Correlated \ac{api} calls are automatically identified, labeled, and consistently grouped using a combination of inference rules and a \acf{ml} model, largely simplifying data consumption. We evaluate \ken's performance, scalability, and expressiveness both in systematic tests and with a series of use cases. We show that it consistently provides accurate context reconstruction, even for complex operations involving 50, 100 or more correlated actions, achieving over 95\% accuracy across the entire spectrum, from simple to highly composite actions.
    %demonstrating its ability to accurately cluster related actions in a scalable and efficient manner.
\end{abstract}

