\begin{abstract} \noindent
We show that the class of Kalmar elementary functions can be inductively generated from the addition, the integer remainder and the base-two exponentiation, hence improving previous results by Marchenkov and Mazzanti. In addition, we further prove that the substitution basis defined by these three operations is minimal.
\\[2 mm]
\textbf{Keywords:} arithmetic term; closed form; elementary function; elementary operation; explicit formula; Grzegorczyk’s hierarchy.
 \\[2 mm]
\textbf{2020 Mathematics Subject Classification:} 03D20 (primary), 03D55, 03B70, 68Q15.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction} \label{introduction}

In this work, we denote by $ \mathbb{N} $ the set of non-negative integers, and by $ \dotdiv $ the \textbf{truncated subtraction}: $ x \dotdiv y := \max( x - y , 0 ) $.

Given an integer $ r \geq 1 $ and a $ r $-ary operation $ f $ on $ \mathbb{N} $, we sometimes write $ f $ as $ ( x_1 , \ldots , x_r ) \mapsto f ( x_1 , \ldots , x_r ) $ or even just as $ f ( x_1 , \ldots , x_r ) $. For example, we sometimes denote the addition as $ x + y $.

In addition, we apply the conventions $ 0^0 = 1 $ (see Mendelson \cite[Proposition 3.16]{mendelson2015introduction}) and $ \floor{x / 0} = 0 $ (see Mazzanti \cite[Section 2.1]{mazzanti2002plainbases}), so, in particular, by applying the definition of the \textbf{integer remainder}, $ x \bmod y := x - y \floor{x / y} $, we have that $ x \bmod 1 = 0 $ and $ x \bmod 0 = x $.

Given a set $ F $ of operations on $ \mathbb{N} $, we denote by $ \langle F \rangle $ the closure under substitution of the set $ F \cup C \cup P $, where $ C $ is the set of constant operations on $ \mathbb{N} $ (which can be identified with non-negative integers) and $ V $ is the set of projections on $ \mathbb{N} $ (which can be identified with variables). And we say that $ F $ is a \textbf{substitution basis} for a given set $ G $ of operations on $ \mathbb{N} $ if, and only if, $ \langle F \rangle = G $ (cf. Mazzanti \cite[Section 2.2]{mazzanti2002plainbases}). For example, if $ S := \langle x + y , \ x^y \rangle $, then $ x^{z + 2} \in S $: indeed, the projection $ ( x , y , z ) \mapsto z $ and the constant operation $ ( x , y , z ) \mapsto 2 $ belong to $ S $, so, because of the closure under substitution of $ S $, the operation $ ( x , y , z ) \mapsto x^{z + 2} $ also belongs to $ S $. Observe that some authors do not admit the constant operations as basic elements in a substitution basis (cf.\ Marchenkov \cite[Introduction]{marchenkov2007superposition}), but, as we are already admitting any variable $ x_n $, it is not unreasonable to also admit any constant $ n $.

The \textbf{Kalmar elementary functions} are the operations on $ \mathbb{N} $ that can be computed in iterated exponential time (cf. Marchenkov \cite[Introduction]{marchenkov2007superposition} and Oitavem \cite[Introduction]{oitavem1997}). They form the class $ \mathcal{E}^3 $ in the Grzegorczyk hierarchy (see Grzegorczyk \cite{grzegorczyk1953someclasses}), which encompasses most of the operations on $ \mathbb{N} $ that frequently arise in mathematical contexts. Notable examples include classical number-theoretic functions such as the factorial function $ n ! $ (see Robinson \cite{robinson1952arithmetic}, Prunescu \& Sauras-Altuzarra \cite{prunescusauras2024factorial}), the $n$-th prime function $ p_n $ (see Prunescu \& Shunia \cite{prunescushunia2024primes}), Euler's totient function $\varphi(n)$ (see Prunescu \& Sauras-Altuzarra \cite{prunescusauras2025manyfunctions}), the greatest common divisor $ \gcd ( m , n ) $ (see Prunescu \& Shunia \cite{prunescushunia2024gcd}), and the C-recursive integer sequences with rational coefficients (see Prunescu \cite{prunescu2025fibonacci2} and Prunescu \& Sauras-Altuzarra \cite{prunescusauras2025fibonacci}). In practical terms, a significant portion of the real-world computation can be faithfully modeled within $ \mathcal{E}^3 $, since the corresponding code typically avoids unbounded recursion and operates within strict time and resource bounds.

For a long time, it was hypothesized that a finite quantity of elementary operations should suffice to form a substitution basis for the whole class of Kalmar elementary functions. Concretely, it was conjectured that $ \mathcal{E}^3 = \langle x + y , \ x \dotdiv y , \ x y , \ \floor{x / y} , \ x^y \rangle $ (see Marchenkov \cite[Introduction]{marchenkov2007superposition}). Drawing upon the foundational contributions of Marchenkov \cite{marchenkov1980superposition} and Matiyasevich \cite{matiyasevich1993hilbert}, Mazzanti \cite{mazzanti2002plainbases} proved this conjecture. Slightly later, Marchenkov \cite[Corollary 2]{marchenkov2007superposition} established that $ \mathcal{E}^3 = \langle x + y , \ x \bmod y , \ x^2 , \ 2^x \rangle $. \cref{MainCorollary} improves Marchenkov's result by eliminating the squaring operation from the above substitution basis, that is to say, it asserts that $ \mathcal{E}^3 = \langle x + y , \ x \bmod y , \ 2^x \rangle $. And \cref{SectionMinimality} is devoted to show that this substitution basis is minimal: if $ F \subsetneqq \{ x + y , x \bmod y , 2^x \} $, then $ \langle F \rangle \subsetneqq \mathcal{E}^3 $. The article concludes in \cref{SectionResearch} by presenting some potential avenues for future research.

\section{A substitution basis for the Kalmar elementary functions}

We start by stating \cref{MarchenkovsTheorem}, which is one of the main results obtained by Marckenkov \cite[Corollary 2]{marchenkov2007superposition}, already mentioned in \cref{introduction}.

\begin{theorem} \label{MarchenkovsTheorem} $ \mathcal{E}^3 = \langle x + y , \ x \bmod y , \ x^2 , \ 2^x \rangle $. \end{theorem}

\cref{thmsquare} shows how to express squaring in terms of the addition, the integer remainder, and the base-two exponentiation.

\begin{theorem} \label{thmsquare} $ x^2 \in \langle x + y , \ x \bmod y , \ 2^x \rangle $. \end{theorem}

\begin{proof} It is clear that $ ( 2^x + x ) \mid ( 2^x - x ) ( 2^x + x ) = 2^{2 x} - x^2 $, so $ 2^{2 x} \equiv x^2 \pmod{2^x + x} $ and, as we have that $ 0 \leq x^2 < 2^x + x $, we have that $$ x^2 = 2^{x + x} \bmod ( 2^x + x ) . $$ \end{proof}

Our main result, \cref{MainCorollary}, is now immediate from \cref{MarchenkovsTheorem} and \cref{thmsquare}.

\begin{cor} \label{MainCorollary} $ \mathcal{E}^3 = \langle x + y , \ x \bmod y , \ 2^x \rangle $. \end{cor}

We conclude this section by providing a proof of \cref{corimportantoperations}, which asserts that the truncated subtraction, the product, and the integer division certainly can be expressed in terms of the elements of the base from \cref{MarchenkovsTheorem}. This result relies in \cref{thmdifference} and \cref{thmquotient}, identities applied by Marchenkov \cite{marchenkov2007superposition} but whose proof appeared to be missing.

\begin{theorem} \label{thmdifference} If $ x $ and $ y $ are non-negative integers, then $$ \left( (2^{x+y} + x) \bmod (2^{x+y} + y) \right) \bmod (2^{x+y}+x) = \begin{cases} x-y & \textup{ if } x \geq y , \\ 0 & \textup{ if } x < y \end{cases} = x \dotdiv y . $$ \end{theorem}

\begin{proof} It is clear that $$ \begin{cases} \left\lbrace \begin{array}{l} 0 \leq x-y < 2^{x+y}+y \\ 2^{x+y}+x = 1 \cdot (2^{x+y}+y) + (x-y) \end{array} \right. & \textup{ if } x \geq y , \\ \left\lbrace \begin{array}{l} 0 \leq 2^{x+y}+x < 2^{x+y}+y \\ 2^{x+y}+x = 0 \cdot (2^{x+y}+y) + (2^{x+y}+x) \end{array} \right. & \textup{ if } x < y , \end{cases} $$ so $$ (2^{x+y}+x) \bmod (2^{x+y}+y) = \begin{cases} x-y & \textup{ if } x \geq y , \\ 2^{x+y}+x & \textup{ if } x < y \end{cases} $$ and, by again applying that $ 0 \leq 2^{x+y}+x < 2^{x+y}+y $ if $ x < y $, the statement follows. \end{proof}

\begin{theorem} \label{thmquotient} If $ x $ and $ y $ are non-negative integers, then $$ \floor{x / y} = \left( 2 ( x + 1 ) \left( x \dotdiv ( x \bmod y ) \right) \right) \bmod ( 2 (x+1) y \dotdiv 1 ) . $$ \end{theorem}

\begin{proof} If $ y = 0 $, then, by the convention mentioned in \cref{introduction}, we have that $ \floor{x / y} = \floor{x / 0} = 0 = 0 \bmod 0 = \left( 2 ( x + 1 ) \left( x \dotdiv x \right) \right) \bmod ( 0 \dotdiv 1 ) = \left( 2 ( x + 1 ) \left( x \dotdiv ( x \bmod 0 ) \right) \right) \bmod ( 2 (x+1) 0 \dotdiv 1 ) = \left( 2 ( x + 1 ) \left( x \dotdiv ( x \bmod y ) \right) \right) \bmod ( 2 (x+1) y \dotdiv 1 ) $. Otherwise, by applying that $ x \dotdiv ( x \bmod y ) = y \lfloor x / y  \rfloor $, we have that $ 2 ( x + 1 ) \left( x \dotdiv ( x \bmod y ) \right) = 2 x y \lfloor x / y \rfloor + 2 y \lfloor x / y \rfloor = \lfloor x / y \rfloor ( 2 x y + 2 y \dotdiv 1 ) + \lfloor x / y \rfloor $ and thus, by applying that $ 0 \leq \lfloor x / y \rfloor \leq x < 2 x y + 2 y \dotdiv 1 = 2 ( x + 1 ) y \dotdiv 1 $, the statement follows. \end{proof}

\begin{cor} \label{corimportantoperations} The operations $ x \dotdiv y $, $ x y $, and $ \floor{x / y} $ belong to $ \langle x + y , \ x \bmod y , \ 2^x \rangle $. \end{cor}

\begin{proof} Let $ S = \langle x + y , \ x \bmod y , \ 2^x \rangle $.

\cref{thmquotient} implies that $ \floor{x / y} \in \langle x + y , \ x \dotdiv y , \ x \bmod y , \ 2 x y \rangle $.

By applying \cref{thmdifference}, we get $ x \dotdiv y \in S $.

Thus, by applying \cref{thmsquare}, we have that $ 2 x y = (x+y)^2 \dotdiv (x^2 + y^2) \in S $ and hence $ \floor{x / y} \in S $.

Finally, observe that $ x y = \floor{2 x y / 2} \in S $. \end{proof}

\section{On minimality} \label{SectionMinimality}

In \cref{MainCorollary}, we have seen that the set $ \{ x + y, x \bmod y, 2^x \} $ is a substitution basis for the Kalmar elementary functions. In this section, we prove that it is, in addition, \textit{minimal}: $ x + y \notin \langle x \bmod y , \ 2^x \rangle $, $ x \bmod y \notin \langle x + y , \ 2^x \rangle $, and $ 2^x \notin \langle x + y , \ x \bmod y \rangle $.

In all three cases, we consider that constants for arbitrary natural numbers can be used in term-building.

\begin{lemma} \label{lemmamodexp} If $ t ( x ) \in \langle x \bmod y , \ 2^x \rangle $, then at least one of the following statements holds.
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Every term of the sequence $ t ( x ) $ is either zero or a power of two.
    \item The sequence $ t ( x ) $ is eventually upper-bounded by $ x $.
\end{enumerate} \end{lemma}

\begin{proof} The proof goes by induction on the elements of $ \langle x \bmod y , \ 2^x \rangle $.

The statement is obvious for the constants and the variable $ x $.

Now, let $ t_1 ( x ) $ and $ t_2 ( x ) $ be elements of $ \langle x \bmod y , \ 2^x \rangle $ whose terms are either zero or powers of two.

In addition, let $ q_1 ( x ) $ and $ q_2 ( x ) $ be elements of $ \langle x \bmod y , \ 2^x \rangle $ that are eventually upper-bounded by $ x $.

Then the terms of the sequences $ 2^{t_1 ( x )} $ and $ 2^{q_1 ( x )} $ are powers of two.

In addition, the terms of $ t_1 ( x ) \bmod t_2 ( x ) $ are either zero or powers of two, because $ x \bmod 0 = x $ (which follows from the convention mentioned in \cref{introduction} that $ \floor{x / 0} = 0 $), $ 0 \bmod x = 0 $, and $$ 2^x \bmod 2^y = \begin{cases} 2^x , & \textrm{ if } x < y , \\ 0 , & \textrm{ if } x \geq y \end{cases} . $$

And the terms of the sequences $ t_1 ( x ) \bmod q_1 ( x ) $, $ q_1 ( x ) \bmod t_1 ( x ) $, and $ q_1 ( x ) \bmod q_2 ( x ) $ are eventually upper-bound by $ x $, because the terms of $ q_1 ( x ) $ are so. \end{proof}

\begin{theorem} $ x + y \notin \langle x \bmod y , \ 2^x \rangle $. \end{theorem}

\begin{proof} Suppose the contrary. It is then clear that $ x + 1 \in \langle x \bmod y , \ 2^x \rangle $. But the sequence $ x + 1 $ has terms that are neither zero nor a power of two, and it is not eventually upper-bounded by $ x $, in contradiction with \cref{lemmamodexp}. \end{proof}

\begin{lemma} \label{lemmastrictlyincreasing} If $ t ( x ) \in \langle x + y , \ 2^x \rangle $ is non-constant, then $ t ( x ) $ is strictly increasing. \end{lemma}

\begin{proof} The proof goes by induction on the elements of $ \langle x + y , \ 2^x \rangle $. The statement is obvious for the constants and the variable $ x $. And, if $ t_1 ( x ) $ and $ t_2 ( x ) $ are strictly increasing elements of $ \langle x + y , \ 2^x \rangle $, then $ t_1 (x) + t_2 (x) $ and $ 2^{t_1 ( x )} $ are also strictly increasing. \end{proof}

\begin{theorem} $ x \bmod y \notin \langle x + y , \ 2^x \rangle $. \end{theorem}

\begin{proof} Suppose the contrary. Then the operation $ x \bmod 2 $, which is not strictly increasing, is also a non-constant element of $ \langle x + y , \ 2^x \rangle $, in contradiction with \cref{lemmastrictlyincreasing}. \end{proof}

\begin{lemma} \label{lemmalineargrowth} If $ t ( x ) \in \langle x + y , \ x \bmod y \rangle $, then $ t ( x ) < A x + B $ for some non-negative integers $ A $ and $ B $. \end{lemma}

\begin{proof} The proof goes by induction on the elements of $ \langle x + y , \ x \bmod y \rangle $. The statement is obvious for the constants and the variable $ x $. And, if $ t_1 ( x ) $ and $ t_2 ( x ) $ are elements of $ \langle x + y , \ x \bmod y \rangle $ such that $t_1(x) < A_1 x + B_1$ and $t_2(x) < A_2x + B_2$ for some non-negative integers $ A_1 $, $ A_2 $, $ B_1 $ and $ B_2 $, then it is clear that $ t_1 (x) + t_2 (x) < (A_1+A_2)x+(B_1+B_2)$ and $ t_1 (x) \bmod t_2 (x) < A_1 x + B_1 $. \end{proof}

\begin{theorem} $ 2^x \notin \langle x + y , \ x \bmod y \rangle $. \end{theorem}

\begin{proof} Suppose the contrary. Then, \cref{lemmalineargrowth} yields the existence of two non-negative integers $ A $ and $ B $ such that $ 2^x < A x + B $, which is clearly impossible. \end{proof}

\section{Open problems} \label{SectionResearch}

It is an interesting question whether set of operations which apparent less computational power, such as $\{2x, \ x \bmod y, \ 2^x\}$ or $\{x+1, \ x \bmod y, \ 2^x\}$, are substitution bases for the class of Kalmar elementary functions.

\begin{lemma} \label{lemmatwomodexp} If $ t ( x ) \in \langle 2 x , \ x \bmod y , \ 2^x \rangle $, then at least one of the following statements holds.
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Every term of the sequence $ t ( x ) $ is either zero or a power of two.
    \item There is an integer $ A \geq 0 $ such that $ t ( x ) $ is eventually upper-bounded by $ A x $.
\end{enumerate} \end{lemma}

\begin{proof} The proof goes by induction on the elements of $ \langle 2 x , \ x \bmod y , \ 2^x \rangle $.

By \cref{lemmamodexp}, we know that the result holds for every element of $ \langle x \bmod y , \ 2^x \rangle $,

Now, let $ t ( x ) \in \langle 2 x , \ x \bmod y , \ 2^x \rangle $.

If every term of the sequence $ t ( x ) $ is either zero or a power of two, then of course every term of the sequence $ 2 t ( x ) $ is also either zero or a power of two.

And, if there is an integer $ A \geq 0 $ such that $ t ( x ) $ is eventually upper-bounded by $ A x $, then $ 2 t ( x ) $ is eventually upper-bounded by $ ( 2 A ) x $. \end{proof}

\begin{proposition} \label{propweakset} $ \langle 2 x , \ x \bmod y , \ 2^x \rangle \subsetneqq \mathcal{E}^3 $. \end{proposition}

\begin{proof} Consider for example the operation $ x^2 $: it belongs to $  \mathcal{E}^3 $, but it has terms that are neither zero nor a power of two, and there is no integer$ A \geq 0 $ for which it is eventually upper-bounded by $ A x $. Hence, by applying \cref{lemmatwomodexp}, $ x^2 $ does not belong to $ \langle 2 x , \ x \bmod y , \ 2^x \rangle $. \end{proof}

We may request that the admissible operations are, in some sense, {\it natural}. This informal condition forces to exclude operations of arity greater than three: indeed, it is not difficult to see that one can build artificial examples of arity three that are not really mathematically relevant, for example
$$ U(x,y,z) := \begin{cases}
    2^x, & \textrm{ if } z = 0,\\
    x+y, & \textrm{ if } z = 1,\\
    x \bmod y, & \textrm{ if } z \geq 2
\end{cases}$$

\begin{proposition} \label{PropBasisArityTypeThree} $ \langle U ( x , y , z ) \rangle = \mathcal{E}^3 $. \end{proposition}

\begin{proof} Let $ S = \langle U ( x , y , z ) \rangle $.

Then $ 2^x = U ( x , y , 0 ) \in S $, $ x + y = U ( x , y , 1 ) \in S $, and $ x \bmod y = U ( x , y , 2 ) \in S $.

Therefore, by applying \cref{MainCorollary}, the statement follows. \end{proof}

Given a positive integer $ k $, we say that a substitution basis for the class of Kalmar elementary functions has \textbf{arity-type} $ \vec{u} \in \mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}^k $ if it consists of $k$ operations whose arities are $u_1$, $u_2$, $\dots$, $u_k$, respectively. For example, \cref{MainCorollary} and \cref{PropBasisArityTypeThree} show that the sets $\{x+y, x\bmod y, 2^x \}$ and $ \{ U ( x , y , z ) \} $ are substitution bases of arity-type $(2,2,1)$ and $(3)$, respectively.

A finite set of unary operations cannot be a substitution basis for the class of Kalmar elementary functions for the simple reason that a term which is built up from unary function-symbols can contain at most one free variable. But, even if we restrict the problem to the class of univariate Kalmar elementary functions, it seems very unlikely that such substitution bases exist.

Our main questions are then the following.
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Decide whether the set $\{x+1, \ x \bmod y, \ 2^x \}$ is a substitution basis for the Kalmar elementary functions.
    \item Decide constructively the existence of substitution bases for the class Kalmar elementary of functions of arity-types $(2,2)$, $(2, 1, 1)$, $(2,1)$, and $(2)$.
    \item Decide constructively the existence of a finite set of unary operations that is a substitution basis for the class of univariate Kalmar elementary functions.
\end{enumerate}

