A point $c\in \sigma$ with $\alpha(c)=\alpha(\sigma)$ is defined as a \emph{center} of the object $\sigma$. Note that the center of an $\alpha$-fat object might not be unique.  If an object $\sigma$ has multiple points satisfying the property of a center, then for our purpose, we can arbitrarily choose any one of them as the center of $\sigma$. 
The minimum (respectively, maximum) {$L_{\infty}$ distance} from the center to the boundary of the object is referred to as the \emph{width} (respectively, \emph{height}) of the object. 
For a fixed $\alpha$, a set $\cal S$ of $\alpha$-fat objects is said to be \emph{similarly sized fat objects} when the ratio of the largest width of an object in $\cal S$ to the smallest width of an object in $\cal S$  is bounded by a fixed constant.












