If the side length of $C$ is $2s(k)$, then that will also work as a side length of $H$. If the side length of $C$ is $s(k)$, we extend it by $\frac{s(k)}{2}$ in both directions to make it of length $2s(k)$, considering it as the side of $H$. Since we are extending it only by $\frac{s(k)}{2}$, it will not contain any new point from $\IZ_k$ apart from the points of $C$. Consequently,  we have $\Q_{s(k)}(H)\subseteq\Q_{s(k)}(\sigma)$. Hence, the claim follows.
\end{proof}


\subsection{Missing Proof of Claim~\ref{clm: r closest point}}\label{App_clm: r closest point}
