%This work focuses on the analysis of a fully discrete mixed finite element method within a Banach space framework for solving nonstationary coupled fluid flow problems governed by the Brinkman-Forchheimer model, with applications to reverse osmosis processes. The mathematical model comprises the coupled unsteady $p$-type convective Brinkman-Forchheimer and transport equations, along with nonlinear mixed boundary conditions across a semi-permeable membrane.
%For the convective Brinkman-Forchheimer equation, we consider a mixed formulation in terms of pseudostress and velocity, while the transport equation is formulated using concentration, its gradient, and a Lagrange multiplier that arises from the reformulation of the membrane boundary condition. The continuous problem is recast in a Banach space setting, rather than a Hilbert space, as an equivalent fixed-point problem, facilitating the well-posedness analysis via the theory of differential-algebraic systems and the Banach fixed-point theorem.
%The spatial discretization of the flux variables, namely the pseudostress and the concentration gradient, employs lowest-order Raviart–Thomas elements, while piecewise constant elements are used for the primal variables, including velocity and concentration. For the Lagrange multiplier, continuous piecewise linear functions are used. A fully discrete Galerkin scheme is then introduced using backward Euler time discretization. The resulting scheme is implicit in the nonlinear terms, and its well-posedness and stability are established using a fixed-point approach similar to that used in the continuous analysis. Finally, convergence in the natural norms for all unknowns is proven, showing optimal rates with respect to both mesh size and time step. Several numerical examples are presented to validate the theoretical error bounds and to demonstrate the performance of the proposed method.
This work analyzes a fully discrete mixed finite element method in a Banach space framework for solving nonstationary coupled fluid flow problems modeled by the Brinkman-Forchheimer equations, with applications to reverse osmosis. The model couples unsteady $p$-type convective Brinkman-Forchheimer and transport equations with nonlinear boundary conditions across a semi-permeable membrane.
A mixed formulation is used for the fluid equation (pseudostress-velocity) and for the transport equation (concentration, its gradient, and a Lagrange multiplier from the membrane condition). The continuous problem is reformulated in Banach spaces as a fixed-point problem, enabling a well-posedness analysis via differential-algebraic system theory.
Spatial discretization employs lowest-order Raviart-Thomas elements for fluxes and piecewise constants for primal variables, while linear elements are used for the Lagrange multiplier. A fully discrete Galerkin scheme with backward Euler time-stepping is proposed. Its well-posedness and stability are proven using a fixed-point argument, and optimal convergence rates are established. Numerical results confirm the theoretical error estimates and demonstrate the method's effectiveness.
\end{abstract}
