\subsection{Well-posedness of the uncoupled problems}\label{sec.2.2}
We aim to show that \( \mathscr{T} \) has a unique fixed point by first proving the well-posedness of the uncoupled problems \eqref{eq:subp1} and \eqref{eq:subp2}, i.e., verifying that \( \mathscr{L}^F \) and \( \mathscr{L}^C \) are well-defined. From the definitions of \( \mathscr{A}^F \) and \( \mathscr{A}^C \), we note that both are elliptic with constants \( \alpha_F := 1/\nu \) and \( \alpha_C := 1/\kappa \), respectively:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:ell.a.at}
		\mathscr{A}^F(\bta , \bta)\, \geq\, \alpha_F \, \Vert \bdev(\bta)\Vert_{0,\Omega}^{2}\quad\forall\,\bta\in \mathbb{H}\qan
		\mathscr{A}^{C}(\bet , \bet)\, \geq\, \alpha_C \, \Vert \bet\Vert_{0,\Omega}^{2}\quad\forall\,\bet\in \mathbf{H}\,. 
\end{equation}

