	\subsubsection{Results and Discussion}
	As shown in Table~\ref{tab:real_world_results}, \textbf{DRAE (ours)} significantly outperforms the static MoE baseline across all evaluated metrics. Specifically, \textbf{DRAE (ours)} achieves a \textbf{13.8\% higher success rate} and requires \textbf{43\% less adaptation time}. Furthermore, it demonstrates \textbf{73.2\% policy transferability}, indicating that the learned experts can successfully generalize to real-world scenarios with minimal degradation in performance. Notably, \textbf{DRAE (ours)} also consumes \textbf{14\% less energy} compared to static MoE, highlighting the energy-efficient nature of the learned models.

	\subsubsection{Failure Cases}
	Despite these improvements, \textbf{DRAE (ours)} encounters difficulties in high-speed dynamic interactions, primarily due to simulation-to-reality discrepancies in force estimation and tactile feedback. Future work will focus on integrating domain adaptation techniques, such as \textbf{RAG} (Recurrent Action Generation) and \textbf{ReflexNet-SchemaPlanner-HyperOptima (RSHO)} for improving the robustness of the model, especially for high-precision control tasks requiring real-time force estimation and multi-modal sensory inputs.

