\begin{abstract}
Generative agents offer promising capabilities for simulating realistic urban behaviors. However, existing methods oversimplify transportation choices in modern cities, and require prohibitive computational resources for large-scale population simulation. To address these limitations, we first present a virtual city that features multiple functional buildings and transportation modes. Then, we conduct extensive surveys to model behavioral choices and mobility preferences among population groups. Building on these insights, we introduce a simulation framework that captures the complexity of urban mobility while remaining scalable, enabling the simulation of over 4,000 agents. To assess the realism of the generated behaviors, we perform a series of micro and macro-level analyses. Beyond mere performance comparison, we explore insightful experiments, such as predicting crowd density from movement patterns and identifying trends in vehicle preferences across agent demographics.


\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Generative agents \cite{uist/ParkOCMLB23}, powered by Large Language Models (LLMs) \cite{emnlp/MadaanZ0YN22}, have emerged as a transformative technology for simulating human-like behaviors in diverse domains, including recommender systems~\cite{sigir/0003CSWC24}, peer review~\cite{bougie2024generative}, medical Q\&A~\cite{corr/abs-2405-02957}, and game simulation~\cite{corr/abs-2309-11672, corr/abs-2404-02039}. A significant application is city simulation~\cite{uist/ParkOCMLB23}, which provides valuable insights for traffic management, public transportation design, and urban planning.

Despite recent advancements~\cite{uist/ParkOCMLB23, emnlp/WangCC23}, existing virtual city simulations still face several challenges in modeling large-scale urban resident behaviors~\cite{kaiya2023lyfe}. These limitations include prohibitive token consumption, computational costs, and unrealistic action planning \cite{feng2024citybench}. Specifically, they fail to account for transport choices and travel duration, lack remote communication between agents, and overlook compulsory daily tasks such as \textit{work} or \textit{sleep}, leading to unrealistic behaviors. In light of this, we introduce, \textbf{MobileCity}, a lightweight simulation platform based on a tile-based city representation. Our framework incorporates diverse urban infrastructures, introduces multiple transportation modes, and enables communication among agents to better reflect contemporary urban mobility. 


To create cost-effective autonomous agents, we leverage LLMs to pre-generate their action spaces and initialize agent profiles using survey-derived \textit{human parameters}. Human parameters capture long-term personality traits and habits of the agent to enable nuanced and diverse behaviors \cite{pmlr-v222-bougie24a}. Additionally, we introduce dynamic \textit{basic needs} --- such as hunger or fatigue, to model evolving internal states that influence agent actions. In our planning module, agent behaviors are determined by both static human parameters and dynamic basic needs while considering compulsory daily tasks. Drawing upon the social brain hypothesis, which posits that significant cognitive capabilities evolved primarily for social relationship management \cite{dunbar2009social}, we implement a novel communication module that enables remote agent interactions, akin to SNS, as well as complex interactions among agents, such as initiating and responding to invitations for in-person meetings.


Experimental evaluations demonstrate that this approach leads to more human-like agent behaviors. Beyond agent-level modeling, we explore the broader implications of \textit{MobileCity} for urban planning and social studies. Through large-scale evaluations, we extract actionable insights at both the individual agent level and the macro city level, offering potential applications in mobility analysis and infrastructure optimization.  





\section{Related Work}
Recent studies in generative agents have demonstrated significant progress in simulating human behavior. \citet{uist/ParkOCMLB23} introduce the first framework for generative agents, enabling them to hold memories and engage in social interactions. Building upon this foundation, \citet{emnlp/WangCC23} introduce basic needs to make agents' daily activities more realistic, while \citet{iclr/ChenSZ0YCYLHQQC24} develop a framework that facilitates emergent collaborative behaviors through customizable environments. To support different application scenarios, \citet{corr/abs-2309-07870} present an open-source framework for autonomous language agents, and \citet{iclr/HongZCZCWZWYLZR24} demonstrate how agents can collaborate in complex software engineering tasks. As research moves towards larger-scale simulations, computational efficiency becomes crucial. \citet{corr/abs-2411-10109} scale up simulations to 1,000 agents by introducing a hierarchical decision-making architecture but inherits prohibitive costs. \citet{corr/abs-2402-02053} propose using learned policies to reduce the cost of repetitive inferences. However, in real-time simulations, these methods remain constrained by the inherent latency of LLM responses, significantly limiting their generation speed. Besides, they do not account for long-term traits characterizing behavior and lack advanced communication among agents. Thus, generating more human-like agent behaviors with lower token consumption remains a challenge. 

