\section{City Simulator}
In our simulation, agents operate in discrete time steps of 15 seconds, during which they perform actions, move between venues, and perceive their environment. To enhance scalability and efficiency, we introduce three key improvements over traditional simulators~\cite{uist/ParkOCMLB23, corr/abs-2402-02053}. First, to accommodate large-scale agent populations our simulator allows stateless simulation, significantly reducing computational overhead. Second, our platform includes diverse buildings and venues, enabling a more comprehensive representation of urban environments. Third, we design a transportation system with multiple mobility hubs, offering agents diverse route options and facilitating realistic citywide movement patterns.

\subsection{City Map}
Our simulated city is constructed using a tile-based map representation, as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:map}. It features a diverse range of urban infrastructures, including residential areas, commercial districts, healthcare facilities, entertainment venues, and transportation hubs. The city consists of 18 buildings: 8 apartment complexes serving as agents' living spaces, 2 office buildings designated for employed agents, and additional structures fulfilling various functional roles. We generate venue descriptions with the input of building names. More details are introduced in Appendix ~\ref{sec:appendix_map}. 


\subsection{Transportation System}
\label{sec:Transportation System}
The diversity of transportation modes facilitates the investigation of mobility patterns among urban residents~\cite{abs-2112-12071}. In our simulation, agents move using three transportation modes: Walking, PMV, and Bus, where PMV refers to a personal mobility vehicle. We introduce a constrained navigation system that dynamically determines optimal routes based on cost, constraints, and individual preferences. Inspired by real-world systems~\cite{wardman2020generalised}, our navigation system generates multiple route options, each differing in time cost and monetary cost. In general, bus routes have the lowest time cost but the highest monetary cost, whereas walking routes are the opposite. To formalize this, we construct three graphs \cite{corr/abs-1809-05481}: a walking graph $G_w$, a PMV graph $G_p$, and a bus graph $G_b$ in our map. Their respective time costs $t_w$, $t_p$, and $t_b$ are calculated as:
\begin{align*}
& t_w = \min_{\pi \in G_w} \text{dist}(s \rightarrow t), \\
& t_p = \min_{\pi \in G_w \cup G_p} \text{dist}(s \rightarrow t), \\
& t_b = \min_{\pi \in G_w \cup G_b} \text{dist}(s \rightarrow t),
\end{align*}
where $s$ is the starting place, $t$ is the terminal place, and $\pi$ represents all the paths in graphs. Route selection is constrained by its upcoming compulsory tasks and influenced by agent group characteristics. For instance, if an agent must reach the office within 15 minutes, it prioritizes the bus to minimize travel time and avoid tardiness. Higher-income agents are more likely to choose PMV due to its time efficiency, whereas lower-income agents may opt for walking or bus to reduce costs. Additionally, weather conditions~\cite{weather} play a crucial role in mobility decisions --- on rainy days, agents tend to avoid PMVs due to safety and comfort concerns. A detailed discussion of agent group attributes is provided in Section~\ref{sec:exp}. 


To mimic human-like decisions when selecting the most suitable route, we constructed a transportation dataset, which consists of survey data collected through an online questionnaire survey of 1,000 respondents. Suppose we aim to compute the probability of an agent choosing ``walking,'' denoted as \( p_w \). Let \( U \) represent the sample data space consisting of users who belong to the same category as the agent and experienced the same weather conditions. To refine our estimation, we retrieve samples with time costs similar to the agent’s planned route:
\begin{align*}
\hat{U} = \text{Top-}n\left\{ u \in U \mid \left\| 
\begin{bmatrix} 
t_w \\ t_p \\ t_b
\end{bmatrix} - 
\begin{bmatrix}
u_{t_w} \\ u_{t_p} \\ u_{t_b}
\end{bmatrix}
\right\|_2 \right\},
\end{align*}
where $\hat{U}$ denotes the retrieved data. 
\begin{align*}
\begin{bmatrix} 
p_w \\ p_p \\ p_b
\end{bmatrix} = 
\begin{bmatrix}
n_w \\ n_p \\ n_b
\end{bmatrix} / (n_w + n_p + n_b),
\end{align*}
where $n_w$, $n_p$, and $n_b$ denote the numbers of respondents~\cite{ml/Sutton88} who choose walking, PMV and bus, respectively. $p_w$, $p_p$ and $p_b$ are possibilities for the agent to choose those modes. More details are introduced in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_transportation}.

\section{Agent Behavior}
MobileCity seeks to generate realistic and scalable agent behaviors that reflect individual decision-making and social interactions. It comprises five major modules: \textbf{profile}, \textbf{action generator}, \textbf{action selector}, \textbf{memory}, and \textbf{communication}.


% To address this, we adopt an offline pre-generation strategy, where an action space is constructed before the simulation begins. During runtime, agents dynamically select behaviors from this pre-defined action space.


\subsection{Agent Profile}
To characterize agents' behavioral preferences, we define two key components: \textbf{human parameters} and \textbf{basic needs}. \textbf{Human Parameters}~\cite{barrick1991big} represent static personality traits and long-term preferences. These parameters, derived from a survey of over 4,000 respondents, are continuous values between 0 and 1. Each parameter is accompanied by a concise description to enhance the LLM’s understanding. \textbf{Basic Needs}~\cite{emnlp/WangCC23} model an agent’s dynamic internal state, evolving throughout the day. Specifically, \textit{Fullness} quantifies satiation based on food and drink consumption, \textit{Happiness} measures emotional well-being and satisfaction, \textit{Health} reflects physical condition and medical status, and \textit{Energy} indicates stamina and capacity for activity. Together, these factors enable nuanced, context-aware behavioral modeling for simulated agents.


\subsection{Action Generator}
We adopt an offline pre-generation strategy, where the action space is constructed before the simulation begins. We employ a chain-of-thought methodology~\cite{nips/Wei0SBIXCLZ22} for LLMs to generate more plausible agent actions. First, we generate venue descriptions for different locations to establish contextual grounding. Second, based on these descriptions, we generate a diverse set of actions while maintaining balance across four action categories: \textit{Meal}, \textit{Entertainment}, \textit{Exercise}, and \textit{Relaxation}. These categories primarily enhance an agent’s \textit{Fullness}, \textit{Happiness}, \textit{Health}, and \textit{Energy}, respectively.  Third, the temporal constraints for each action are determined by specifying the earliest start time and latest end time, ensuring temporal consistency. Finally, we define the impact of each action on the agent’s basic needs after completion. Appendix~\ref{sec:agent_behavior} provides further details on examples.

\subsection{Action Selector}
At execution, agents dynamically select actions from this pre-defined action space. At the start of each day, agents are assigned compulsory tasks, which fall into three categories: \textbf{sleep}, \textbf{work}, and \textbf{invitation}. All agents are required to sleep at night, ensuring adherence to natural rest cycles. Employees must attend work during designated hours on weekdays. Additionally, social interactions and invitations introduce scheduled commitments, requiring agents to honor their appointments with others (see Section~\ref{sec:communication_module}).

Action selection involves first choosing a category based on current \textit{basic needs}, filtering out actions that conflict with compulsory tasks, then ranking remaining actions based on their similarity with the agent's \textit{human parameters}, forming a probability distribution over the remaining choices. This is formalized as follows:
\begin{align*}
\hat{A} = \text{Top-}5\left\{ a \in A \mid \left\| 
sim(hp_a, hp_u)
\right\|_2 \right\},
\end{align*}
where $A$ refers to the action space, $hp_a$ and $hp_u$ are the human parameters of actions and agents, and $\hat{A}$ indicates the set of top-5 actions. Given $\hat{A}$, the likelihood of choosing the action $a$, $p_a$ is then expressed as:
\begin{align*}
p_a = \frac{sim(hp_a, hp_u)}{\sum_{a' \in \hat{A}} sim(hp_{a'}, hp_u)} \quad \forall a \in \hat{A}.
\end{align*}
Once an action is selected, the agent determines a transportation mode following the process outlined in Section~\ref{sec:Transportation System}.


