\subsection{Memory Module}
The memory module~\cite{corr/abs-2502-12110} serves as the agent's episodic memory, retaining past interactions and dialogues to enable consistent decision-making and long-term adaptation. Inspired by cognitive memory models~\cite{anderson1991reflections}, it organizes experiences in a structured format, allowing the agent to retrieve relevant context efficiently. Each interaction, including dialogues, observations, and agent decisions, is stored as a memory entry. The memory module accumulates a history of agent experiences over time, facilitating reasoning over past events and enabling the agent to refine its responses based on prior encounters.


\subsection{Interactive Communication Module}
\label{sec:communication_module}

In our simulated city, communication is facilitated through both face-to-face and mobile interactions. 

\textbf{Face-to-face communication} occurs when two agents encounter each other within the same space. We define \textit{relationship scores} --- affinity and familiarity, that quantify the strength of connections between agents. When an agent enters a new location, it selects its preferred conversation partner based on these relationship scores, mimicking social relationship management in the human brain \cite{dunbar2009social}.

\textbf{Mobile communication} enables agents to interact remotely via phone calls. We introduce the concept of \textit{Sociality} to measure an agent's satisfaction with its social interactions. If an agent's \textit{Sociality} level falls below a threshold, it desires social connection~\cite{epley2008creating} and may initiate a call to a friend. When the two agents talk to each other, one agent may invite another agent to do something together. In this case, an invitation task is added to their personal calendars and will be executed on the corresponding date. We model the probability of agent $u$ picking agent $v$ as a conversation partner as:
\begin{align*}
p_v = \frac{r(u, v)}{\sum_{v' \in V} r(u, v')} \quad \forall v \in V,
\end{align*}
where $r(u, v)$ is the relationship score between agents and $V$ is the set of agents who agent $u$ is able to talk to. After each interaction, relationship scores are updated to reflect changes in social bonds.
