\subsection{Emotion Monitoring}

To analyze agents' emotional states, we categorize them into three employment statuses and compute the average values of their basic needs over five weekdays. Figure~\ref{fig:basic_needs} illustrates the variations in these attributes. The attributes fluctuate least for unemployed agents, moderately for part-time workers, and most dramatically for employed agents. All attributes, except \textit{Fullness}, follow a consistent pattern: a steady decline between 9:00 and 18:00, followed by recovery during non-working hours. This trend arises because employed agents are predominantly occupied with work during the day, limiting their ability to engage in restorative activities. They can only replenish their basic needs after completing compulsory tasks. In contrast, \textit{Fullness} increases around 8:00, 12:00, and 18:00, as agents tend to eat meals during these typical mealtimes.



\section{Conclusions}
This work presents a scalable framework for large-scale agent-based simulation in dynamic urban environments. We introduce a novel city simulator, incorporating a novel transportation system that enhances movement diversity and realism. In addition, we propose an agent architecture where agents select actions based on their static \textit{human parameters}, dynamic \textit{basic needs}, and compulsory tasks. We further introduce remote and in-person communication to enable human-like communication. As a result, MobileCity enhances agent realism while maintaining computational feasibility. The collected insights are intended to improve traffic safety and urban planning, reducing reliance on costly real-world data.



