\section{City Simulator}
\subsection{City Map}
\label{sec:appendix_map}
Our simulated city contains 18 buildings, as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:buildings},  and 68 venues, as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:rooms}. The city features 8 apartment complexes, 2 company offices, 5 parks, 1 hospital, 1 department store, and 1 stadium. Each building contains different spaces. For example, an apartment building contains several living spaces for agents, 1 restaurant, and 1 convenience store or 1 entertainment venue. A company building contains several offices, 1 company canteen, and 1 convenience store.

\subsection{Transportation System}
\label{sec:appendix_transportation}
The city map follows a grid-based structure where each tile represents 25 meters, and each block spans 500 meters. At each simulation time step, agents traverse 1, 2, or 5 tiles depending on whether they are walking, using a PMV, or taking a bus, respectively. Consequently, the time required to travel across a full block is 300, 150, and 60 seconds for walking, PMV, and bus travel, respectively. 

We constructed 3 graphs: a walking graph $G_w$, a PMV graph $G_p$, and a bus graph $G_b$ in our map. Each graph is constructed with nodes representing accessible points for agents, and edges representing different moving costs.


A walking graph consists of passages inside buildings, which are yellow areas in Figure~\ref{fig:walking_graph}, and zebra crossings between buildings in Figure~\ref{fig:PMV_graph}. In one building, agents can access most of the areas except for collision walls. Between buildings, agents can walk across zebra crossings on highways. An agent moves 1 tile in each time step by walking.


A PMV graph consists of nodes of PMV stations, represented as blue tiles in Figure~\ref{fig:PMV_graph}. To ride a PMV, agents must walk to the PMV station first, then ride the PMV on the left side of the highway. An agent moves 2 tiles in each time step when using a  PMV.

