\section{Agent Behavior}
\label{sec:agent_behavior}
We now provide a comprehensive explanation of Agent Behavior, detailing the implementation and technical details. In the 30-agent experiment, we use the five modules to model agent behaviors. However, in the 4000-agent experiment, the conversation and memory modules are disabled to optimize computational efficiency.

\subsection{Action Selector}
This is a detailed example of the action selector. Now, it is 7:00 AM on Monday, and our agent Alex Kim wakes up. He will select an action by following these steps.

Step 1: Choose an action category based on the \textit{Basic Needs}. As described in Figure~\ref{fig:basic_needs2}, Alex feels hungry, so he will choose a Meal action. There are 4 Meal actions in Figure~\ref{fig:filter_time}.

Step 2: Filter out actions that do not meet execution time constraints. For the action ``have lunch in the restaurant'', it is too early to have lunch in the morning. For the action ``have breakfast in the company'', Alex would not be able to start working in the office at 9:00 AM punctually if he does so, so this action is also filtered out due to a \textit{compulsory task}.

Compulsory tasks are added to agent calendars when a day starts. There are 3 categories of compulsory tasks in our experiments: sleep, work, and invitation. Every agent has to sleep at night. Employee agents have to go to work during designated working hours on weekdays. Additionally, due to social interactions and invitations between agents, they must honor their commitments and appointments with others.

Step 3: Calculate the similarities between agents' human parameters and the actions, as described in Figure~\ref{fig:filter_hp}.. Human parameters for actions are generated before the experiment. After normalization, the possibility for Alex to choose ``have breakfast at home'' is 0.8, while ``have breakfast in the convenience store'' is 0.2. If we want to generate actions carefully based on agent characters, we could use LLM to decide. However, in a large-scale experiment, the LLM is shut down, and the probability distribution of actions is determined by the similarities.


Step 4: Decide the transportation mode.

\subsection{Dataset Description}

Our proprietary dataset is derived from a survey of over 4,000 respondents and contains continuous human parameters, ranging between 0 and 1. Human parameters capture personality and lifestyle traits. In addition, our dataset includes detailed daily activity schedules for each individual, specifying the modes of transportation used for different activities. These real-world schedules serve as a benchmark to assess the faithfulness of our proposed simulation framework, ensuring that it accurately reflects human behavioral patterns.

