\noindent Many deep learning (DL)-based approaches for scientific, engineering, and social problems involve cross-scenario generalization, i.e., adapting to a new scenario based on the model learned in some other different but related scenarios\citeup{deep_learning,dl_book}. 
For example, in weather forecasting, we usually attempt to adapt the model learned for some cities or seasons to a new city or another season, whereas the differences between cities or seasons have significant impacts on the meteorological patterns\citeup{weather_model1,weather_model2}. Similarly, in financial forecasting, models are trained on a diverse set of financial datasets and are desired to adapt to new market conditions\citeup{economy_metalearning}. As a prevailing example in the engineering field, intelligent wireless localization, needs to deal with the diverse scenarios of wireless cellular networks, wherein the complex scattering environment and system settings of each cell yield quite different electromagnetic characteristics\citeup{wBAIM}, as shown in Fig. \ref{overview_fig}(a). For DL models applied in such problems, robust cross-scenario generalization capability is therefore imperative.


