%\subsection*{Learning with Implicit Scenario Embedding}
\subsection*{Implicit Scenario Embedding}
Inspired by ${\mathrm{g}^*}\left( {{S^*},\cdot} \right)$, we conjectured the feasibility of learning multi-scenario generalization. However, due to the non-characterizability of  $S^*$, directly using $NN\left( {{S^*},\cdot} \right)$ as a learning objective is infeasible. To address this, we introduce a relaxed approach: instead of taking $S^*$ as input, the neural network can leverage information that is deterministically related to $S^*$, thereby implicitly capturing the necessary scenario-specific characteristics.

As introduced in the 'Problem Formulation' Section, the scenario-specific function ${g_S}(\cdot)$ is directly conditioned on $S^*$. Furthermore, ${g_S}(\cdot)$ can be fitted using data-label pairs from the studied scenario, thereby these pairs can be an effective means to implicitly characterize $S^*$. Based on these insights, we formulate the following learning objective:
\begin{align}
    &NN\left( {({\mathbf{H}}_j ^i,{\bm{x}}_j ^i)_{j \in \Theta},{\mathbf{H}}} \right) \to {\bm{x}}, \label{eq_new_paradigm} \\ \nonumber
    %&i \in \left\{ {1, \ldots ,k} \right\},
    &\Theta=\{j_1,\ldots,j_n\}  \subseteq \left\{ {1, \ldots ,{l_i}} \right\}.
\end{align}
The user is in scenario $i$, i.e., $\mathbf{H}$ belongs to scenario $i$. Here and subsequently, we directly use $\mathbf{H}$ and $\bm{x}$ instead of $A$ and $B$, respectively, to maintain consistency in the narrative. Also, the $({\mathbf{H}}_j ^i,{\bm{x}}_j ^i)$ includes not only the state values, but also the correspondences. In this objective, the reasoning about $\bm{x}$ no longer relies solely on $\mathbf{H}$ but also incorporates the embedded pairs $({\mathbf{H}}_j ^i,{\bm{x}}_j ^i)$ to perceive the characteristics of the scenario, as illustrated in Fig. \ref{mateformer_fig}(a).  This design allows the trained $NN(\cdot)$ to dynamically adjust its inference process according to the scenario information and thus adapt to the variability among scenarios. From another perspective, \eqref{eq_new_paradigm} employs known reference objects as substitutes for reference frames that are hard to characterize. By incorporating awareness of reference frames, the learned feature transformations more closely align with the underlying mechanism, enabling broader and more robust generalization.


\subsection*{Relativity-Based Analogy}
In fact, the form of \eqref{eq_new_paradigm} is similar to ICL\citeup{in_context_survey}. However, ICL is an unfolded form $NN\left( {{\mathbf{H}}_{{j_1}}^i,{\bm{x}}_{{j_1}}^i, \ldots ,{\mathbf{H}}_{{j_n}}^i,{\bm{x}}_{{j_n}}^i,{\mathbf{H}}} \right) \to {\bm{x}}$ and annotates the correspondence between ${\mathbf{H}}_j ^i$ and ${\bm{x}}_j ^i$ using language descriptions or positional encoding. Representing the correspondence through annotations rather than structural methods is less precise, hindering the model's ability to fully exploit these relationships and limiting its final performance. The `Appendix' provides detailed experimental comparisons between ICL and AL, illustrating this point. The loose design of ICL's annotation-based approach arises from its ambiguity regarding the role of embedded samples in inference. By contrast, AL clearly defines these samples as reference objects. In other words, all samples (whether embedded or to be inferred) are relative information rather than isolated entities. This perspective of relativity leads to a new idea: using relative weights as a medium to fully exploit the key insight that the reference frames are shared between samples.


Specifically, we perform a transformation to \eqref{eq_new_paradigm}. 
We approach it by implicitly representing $\mathbf{H}$ through its relative relationships with ${\mathbf{H}}_j^i$, and then aligning these relationships to the label space to guide the reconstruction of $\bm{x}$ from ${\bm{x}}_j^i$. This process is illustrated in Fig. \ref{mateformer_fig}(b) and can also be formulated as follows:
\begin{equation}
    N{N_{{\rm{use\_rela}}}}{\left( {N{N_{{\rm{ext\_rela}}}}({\bf{H}}_j^i,{\bf{H}}),{\bf{x}}_j^i} \right)_{j \in \Theta }} \to \bm{x},
\label{eq_al}
\end{equation}
where \( NN_{{\rm{ext\_rela}}}(\cdot) \) refers to the partial network used to extract the relative information among \( ({\bf{H}}_j^i, {\bf{H}}) \), and \( NN_{{\rm{use\_rela}}}(\cdot) \) represents another part of the network that couples the extracted relative information with \( {\bf{x}}_j^i \).
This design incorporates prior constraints to facilitate learning. 
On one hand, it decouples the correspondence between $\mathbf{H}_j^i$ and $\bm{x}_j^i$, transforming the problem of cross-attribute characterizing $\mathbf{H}_j^i \to \bm{x}_j^i$ into a more tractable intra-attribute interaction problem, namely the characterizing relationships between $\mathbf{H}$ and $\mathbf{H}_j^i$ as well as $\bm{x}$ and ${\bm{x}}_j^i$. 
On the other hand, dimensionless relative information has significant advantages in cross-attribute transfer. As a result, aligning relativity can be accurately learned by neural networks, thereby capturing the relational equivalence between $\mathbf{H} \to \bm{x}$ and $\mathbf{H}_j^i \to \bm{x}_j^i$.

\subsection*{Mateformer: The Neural Network Structure}
The attention mechanism\citeup{attention} provides an effective approach for extracting relative weights among data and guiding the synthesis of new data. Therefore, based on \eqref{eq_al}, we design a learning network named Mateformer, whose structure is illustrated in Fig. 2(c). Structurally, Mateformer comprises two Transformer\citeup{transformer} modules, referred to as Transformer\_$\mathbf{H}$ and Transformer\_$\mathbf{H}\bm{x}$ (sharing the same width), along with several linear layers for dimensional transformation. 

The inputs and output of Mateformer are as follows. The inputs to Mateformer are sequences $Seq\_\mathbf{H}$ and $Seq\_\bm{x}$, which maintain the element-wise correspondence in order. Here, $Seq\_\mathbf{H}$ consists of $\mathbf{H}$ and $\mathbf{H}_j^i~(j\in\Theta)$, while $Seq\_\bm{x}$ is composed of ${\bm{x}}_\mathrm{init}$ and $\bm{x}_j^i~(j\in\Theta)$. Since the true $\bm{x}$ is to be solved, ${\bm{x}}_\mathrm{init}$ can be either predefined or learnable parameters. Besides, learnable embeddings are added in Transformer\_$\mathbf{H}\bm{x}$ to indicate whether the input is a predefined ${\bm{x}}_\mathrm{init}$ or a known $\bm{x}_j^i$. In this work, we use the mean value of $\bm{x}_j^i$ as ${\bm{x}}_\mathrm{init}$. Moreover, in training, in order to facilitate parallelization, $\mathbf{H}$ can be not just a single sample but a sequence of multiple to-be-inferred samples in the studied scenario. In Mateformer, the masking is applied to the $\mathbf{H}$ and ${\bm{x}}_\mathrm{init}$ parts of the two sequences, respectively, ensuring that the evaluation of relativity and the synthesis process are only based on the accurate known samples ${\mathbf{H}}_j^i$ and ${\bm{x}}_j^i$. The final output of Mateformer is the output of ${\bm{x}}_\mathrm{init}$ from the Transformer\_$\mathbf{H}\bm{x}$, namely the inferred location $\widehat{\bm{x}}$.

Functionally, the stacked layers of Transformer\_$\mathbf{H}$ continuously perform interactions and transformations within $Seq\_\mathbf{H}$, and also share the output of $k$-th layer ${Seq^k}\_\mathbf{H}$ to the $(k+1)$-th layer of Transformer\_$\mathbf{H}\bm{x}$. Transformer\_$\mathbf{H}\bm{x}$ then guides the interactions within ${Seq^k}\_\bm{x}$ based on the attention weight of ${Seq^k}\_\mathbf{H}$. This process can be formulated as follows:
\begin{align}
{Seq^{k + 1}}\_{\bf{H}} &= {\rm{FFN}^{k+1}_{\bf{H}}}\left( {{\rm{MHead}^{k+1}_{\bf{H}}}\left( {Se{q^k}\_{\bf{H}},Se{q^k}\_{\bf{H}},Se{q^k}\_{\bf{H}}} \right)} \right), \label{eq_transformer_H} \\ 
{Seq^{k + 1}}\_{\bm{x}} &= {{\rm{FFN}}^{k+1}_{\mathbf{H} \bm{x}}}\left( {{{\rm{MHead}}^{k+1}_{\mathbf{H} {\bm{x}}}}\left( {Se{q^k}\_{\bf{H}},Se{q^k}\_{\bf{H}},Se{q^k}\_{\bm{x}}} \right)} \right), \label{eq_transformer_Hx}
\\
k&=0,1,\dots,N-1, \nonumber
\end{align}
where $\rm{FFN}$ is the feedforward network, $\rm{MHead}$ is the multi-head attention module, and to highlight the key ideas, the used residual computation and layer normalization are omitted here, with the complete computational flow illustrated in Fig. \ref{mateformer_fig}(c). The design rationale is as follows: each layer of Transformer\_$\mathbf{H}\bm{x}$ computes the relative relationships between $\mathbf{H}$ and $\mathbf{H}_j^i$, and uses this to guide the synthesis of $\bm{x}$, thereby fulfilling  the main objective of \eqref{eq_al}. Since \eqref{eq_transformer_Hx} only outputs new ${Seq}\_{\bm{x}}$ and does not produce new ${Seq}\_{\bf{H}}$, we introduce a mate module, Transformer\_$\mathbf{H}$, to continuously transform the ${Seq}\_{\bf{H}}$, enabling the evaluation of relativity in multiple feature spaces. 
This design enables stackability of the learning network, allowing analogies in DL to transcend beyond pre-defined algorithms\citeup{AL_try1} or single-layer sub-function modules\citeup{AL_try2} and permeate throughout the entire learning process. Moreover, it maintains the model's deep nonlinear representational capacity without requiring additional algorithms or modules.
The training of Mateformer is based on the MSELoss function and optimized using a gradient descent-based optimizer, with Adam\citeup{adam} being employed in this work.

