\bmsection{Appendix} \label{appendix}
\subsection{Backgrounds and related works of intelligent wireless localization} \label{appendix_background}
Wireless localization refers to inferring user location based on the user's wireless fingerprint, such as CSI between user and BS. Compared to GNSS, wireless localization  offers inherent advantages in both precision and real-time performance, owing to shorter signal transmission distances and the inclusion of phase information\citeup{cooper_loc}. 
Recently, the accuracy of wireless localization is improving significantly empowered by AI\citeup{wireless_loc_survey}. Using neural networks to learn a mapping function from wireless fingerprint to location, intelligent localization has allowed centimeter-level accuracy based on high-density measurement data\citeup{ad_cnn1}. Moreover, researchers further explored numerous methods to optimize the accuracy\citeup{ad_cnn2,ad_cnn2_long,ad_cnn3_long,cnn4,mccnet}, improve system compatibility\citeup{fdma_positioning}, and reduce the cost of measurement\citeup{transloc1,transloc2,metaloc}.

To address cross-scenario performance degradation in wireless AI, both initialization-based and feature-based cross-scenario model reuse methods have been studied. Leveraging transfer learning\citeup{transfer_learning} or meta-learning\citeup{meta_learning} techniques, some works improve the parameter initialization of LNN training to accelerate the learning process in new scenarios\citeup{transloc1,transloc2,meta_learning}. Meanwhile, multi-task learning\citeup{multi_task} has also been introduced into wireless AI research by treating different scenarios as different tasks\citeup{multi_task_csinet}, which is also a potential means to optimize intelligent localization. By splitting the LNN into backbone and head, the backbone part is shared across scenarios and the head changes with the scenarios, so as to reduce the learning complexity in new scenarios. However, these methods still require training in new scenarios to tune the learned intelligence, which mitigates but cannot fundamentally resolve issues like training hardware cost and latency.

\subsection{Differences between multi-scenario generalization and domain adaptation or domain generalization} \label{appendix_domainadaption}
In domain adaptation or generalization, the challenge lies in the discrepancy between the data distribution \(P_i\) of the source domain \(\mathbf{D}_i\) and the data distribution \(P_j\) of the target domain \(\mathbf{D}_j\) (\(i \neq j\)), which leads to performance degradation of models trained on \(P_i\) when applied to \(P_j\). However, in such problems, the  function \(\mathrm{g}(\cdot): A \rightarrow B\) remains invariant across domains (from \(\mathbf{D}_i\) to \(\mathbf{D}_j\)). The performance drop arises because the neural network fails to accurately approximate \(g(\cdot)\), instead overfitting to the distribution \(P_i\). This is fundamentally different from the multi-scenario generalization problem studied in this article, where \(\mathrm{g}(\cdot)\) varies across scenarios.

The invariance of \(g(\cdot)\) makes domain adaptation or domain generalization problems more tractable. On the one hand, the distribution differences between \(\mathbf{D}_i\) and \(\mathbf{D}_{i+1}\) do not hinder joint learning of the shared \(g(\cdot)\) from both domains, and such joint learning often leads to a more effective approximation of \(\mathrm{g}(\cdot)\). However, in multi-scenario generalization, \(\mathrm{g}_{s_i}(\cdot)\) and \(\mathrm{g}_{s_{i+1}}(\cdot)\) cannot be reused interchangeably, making it impossible to fully share the learning process between them. On the other hand, the feasibility of joint learning also facilitates generalization to new distributions. A sufficiently diverse training set \(\mathbf{D}_1, \ldots, \mathbf{D}_k\) often provides an approximate coverage on the new dataset \(\mathbf{D}_{k+1}\), enabling high-efficiency generalization. The recent success of numerous large language models reflects this point. However, in multi-scenario generalization, even if \(\mathrm{g}_{s_1}(\cdot), \ldots, \mathrm{g}_{s_k}(\cdot)\) have been fitted by neural networks, these complex black-box results cannot form effective ensemble akin to data distributions to meet the functional requirements of \(\mathrm{g}_{s_{k+1}}(\cdot)\). Therefore, multi-scenario generalization is an important and highly challenging problem.

