\section{Introduction}
\IEEEPARstart{F}{or} the past 25 years, channel access in IEEE 802.11 (Wi-Fi) has been based on fully distributed random access.
With network densification, this `no-coordination' approach no longer meets the requirements of recent applications (such as augmented reality).
The first improvement in this area was the introduction of intra-basic service set (BSS) coordination with orthogonal frequency-division multiple access (OFDMA) in 802.11ax (Wi-Fi 6), where the access point (AP) coordinates both uplink (UL) and downlink (DL) transmissions. 
The next step is 802.11bn inter-BSS coordination, where neighboring APs coordinate their transmissions for interference management.

