\begin{abstract}
Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) is a mainstream method for improving performance on knowledge-intensive tasks. However, current RAG systems often place too much emphasis on retrieved contexts. This can lead to reliance on inaccurate sources and overlook the model's inherent knowledge, especially when dealing with misleading or excessive information. To resolve this imbalance,  we propose \method that using joint sampling and define multi policy distributions in knowledge capability exploration to stimulate large language models' self-integrated utilization of parametric and contextual knowledge. Experiments show that \method significantly enhances robustness and reasoning accuracy in both parameters and contextual conflict tasks and general RAG tasks, especially outperforming baselines by 17.07\% in counterfactual scenarios and demonstrating consistent gains across RAG tasks.Our code are available at \url{https://github.com/lcy80366872/knowledgeable-r1}.


\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

% 用 windows 改成 pdf 格式
\begin{figure*}[!t]%
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{images/introduction-new.pdf}
    \caption{LLMs are prone to rely on completeness and conflict to judge the accuracy of article facts. When faced with conflicting or misleading retrieval content, LLMs ignore known information, which reduces reasoning accuracy. }\label{fig:introduction}
    \vspace{-1em}

\end{figure*}


Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) has become an effective strategy for knowledge-intensive tasks \cite{nakano2021webgpt,gao2023retrieval}. Current research reveals that LLMs exhibit a strong preference toward contextual knowledge over parametric knowledge \cite{NEURIPS2024_baf4b960,xie2024adaptivechameleonstubbornsloth}. This preference becomes problematic in scenarios involving conflicting knowledge or contextual inconsistencies \cite{lee2024one,10.1145/3637528.3671458,sun2024exploring,wang2024astute, tan-etal-2024-blinded}, often resulting in erroneous outputs as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:introduction}. Therefore, enabling large models to effectively integrate between parameter and contextual knowledge remains a critical challenge.\\

Recent studies propose contextual misinformation discrimination mechanisms that shift faith to parametric knowledge when detecting context inaccuracies \cite{das2023multi,upadhyay2024addressing,torreggiani2025dont}. The reliability of modern LLMs' parametric knowledge is growing, which makes this approach effective \cite{mallen2023not,yang2024harnessing}. However, current implementations face distinct challenges across three primary directions. Prompt-guided techniques \cite{pan2023risk,zhou2023context,wang2024astute,xu2024knowledge,ying2024intuitive} alert models to potential inaccuracies through warning prompts, though they often struggle with inconsistent sensitivity in real-world applications. Another strategy \cite{xu2024knowledge,williams-etal-2018-broad,cheng2023distinguishing,zhang2024comprehensive} employs question-augmented frameworks that refine retrieval accuracy by rephrasing queries multiple times, but this iterative process creates heavy computational overhead during inference . The third category \cite{hong2024so,ju2025investigating,jin2024tug} enhances detection through specialized training modules , achieving better discrimination at the cost of increased memory demands and operational complexity. While these approaches improve knowledge conflict recognition from different angles, they generally face efficiency-performance trade-offs \cite{mu2021role,su2024conflictbank,xu2024knowledge,wang2023resolving}. However, few study focuses on exploring  LLM's abilities in solving the contextual and parametric knowledge conflict problems without using extra models or components.




To address this limitation, we propose a reinforcement learning framework \method that enhances models' capability to judiciously integrate parametric and contextual knowledge through factual grounding. While existing RL methods like GRPO \cite{shao2024deepseekmath}  enable contextual knowledge utilization, they neglect systematic exploration of parametric knowledge. Our method introduces two key components: knowledge capability exploration and optimization. The knowledge exploration allows the model to systematically probe its parametric knowledge, while knowledge capability optimization guide balanced use of contextual knowledge. This dual design enables the model to explore both contextual knowledge and parametric simultaneously, ensuring decisions align with factual accuracy. 


We evaluate \method fact conflict resolution and QA capabilities in RAG scenarios. By implementing reinforcement learning to explore parametric knowledge and contextual knowledge paths, \method  achieves an average accuracy improvement of \textbf{8.39\%} on ConflictQA dataset\cite{bi2024context}, which involves counterfactual contextual knowledge and self-contradictory contextual knowledge. Notably, the improvement achieves \textbf{9.12\%} enhancement with correct contextual context and reaches \textbf{17.07\%} even contextual knowledge is error.
\section{RELATED WORK}
\subsection{Parametric and Contextual Knowledge in LLMs}
Large Language Models (LLMs) store a substantial quantity of parametric knowledge post-training, but their ability to access and use this knowledge effectively varies \cite{inui2019proceedings,hu2023survey,gueta2023knowledge}. The integration of too much contextual information can cause LLMs to forget previously learned knowledge, known as catastrophic forgetting \cite{wen2024mindmap,wang2025bring}. LLMs may suppress their parametric knowledge in the presence of contextual cues, even when it's beneficial \cite{cheng2024understandinginterplayparametriccontextual}. Studies have tried combining parametric knowledge with contextual data, but this process can be inefficient and prone to errors \cite{jeong2024adaptive,fan2024survey}.
\subsection{Reinforcement Learning LLM Reasoning}
OpenAI's O1 model marked a significant advancement in LLMs by incorporating extended reasoning \cite{jaech2024openai,zelikman2024quiet,guan2024deliberative,shao2024deepseekmath}. The DeepSeek R1 model made its training approach and weights public, showing similar capabilities to O1 \cite{guo2025deepseek}. DAPO addressed specific challenges in LLM RL extensions, such as entropy collapse, and suggested solutions to improve performance \cite{yu2025dapo}. The Search-R1 model showed LLMs using RL to generate search queries through reasoning\cite{jin2025search}. However, these methods are limited by the prompts, with low-probability outputs, like parametric knowledge during RAG reasoning, often overlooked due to static prompts. 

\section{Method}
\subsection{Task Definition}
Given an original prompt   $p$ and and its retrieval-augmented prompt $p'$  , when processed by a large language model (LLM), they produce outputs $o$  and $o'$  respectively, with $o^*$ representing the correct response. Our goal is to develop the LLM policy $\pi_\theta(o^*|p')$ that can intelligently integrate parametric knowledge and contextual knowledge, ultimately generating accurate responses. A simplified overview of our method is provided in Algorithm \ref{algdepo}.The prompt representations are in Appendix \ref{prompt:appendix}. 




\begin{algorithm*}[!t]
\small
\caption{\method: Policy Optimization for Knowledge Exploration}
\label{algdepo}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Statex \textbf{Input:} Current policy $\pi_{\theta}$, old policy $\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}$, dataset $\mathcal{D}$, training steps $Step_{\text{max}}$, parametric knowledge prompting rollout number $n_1$, contextual knowledge prompt rollout number $n_2$, clip parameter $\epsilon$, advantage transformation function $T(\cdot)$,parametric knowledge prompt $p$, contextual knowledge prompt $p'$ .
\Statex \textbf{Output:} Updated policy $\pi_{\theta}$
\For{$s = 1$ to $Step_{\text{max}}$}
    \State Sample batch $(p) \sim \mathcal{D}$
    \State Sample batch $(p') \sim \mathcal{D}$   
    \State Sample $\{o_i\}_{i=1}^{n_1}$ from $\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}(o \mid p)$   \Comment{Contextual knowledge rollouts}
    \State Sample $\{o'_j\}_{j=1}^{n_2}$ from $\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}(o' \mid p')$ \Comment{Parametric knowledge rollouts}
    \State Compute advantages $A_{i}$ , $A'_{j}$ and $\hat{A_i}$ for all rollouts

    \State Define $l$ as the  parametric  knowledge based-policy objective:
    \vspace{-1em}
    \begin{equation*}
    l(\theta) = \frac{1}{n_1} \sum_{i=1}^{n_1} \sum_{t=1}^{|o_{i}|} \min \left[\frac{\pi_{\theta}\left(o_{i,t} \mid p, o_{i,<t}\right)}{\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}\left(o_{i,t} \mid p, o_{i,<t}\right)} A_{i}, \operatorname{clip}\left(\frac{\pi_{\theta}\left(o_{i,t} \mid p, o_{i,<t}\right)}{\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}\left(o_{i,t} \mid p, o_{i,<t}\right)}; 1-\epsilon, 1+\epsilon\right) A_{i}\right]
    \end{equation*}
    \vspace{-1em}
    \State Define $l'$ as the contextual knowledge based-policy objective:
    \vspace{-1em}
    \begin{equation*}
    l'(\theta) = \frac{1}{n_2} \sum_{j=1}^{n_2} \sum_{t=1}^{|o'_{j}|} \min \left[\frac{\pi_{\theta}\left(o'_{j,t} \mid p', o'_{j,<t}\right)}{\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}\left(o'_{j,t} \mid p', o'_{j,<t}\right)} A'_{j}, \operatorname{clip}\left(\frac{\pi_{\theta}\left(o'_{j,t} \mid p', o'_{j,<t}\right)}{\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}\left(o'_{j,t} \mid p', o'_{j,<t}\right)}; 1-\epsilon, 1+\epsilon\right) A'_{j}\right]
    \end{equation*}
    \vspace{-1em}
   \State Define $\hat{l}$ as the parametric knowledge reasoning policy objective with contextual knowledge input :
   \vspace{-1em}
    \begin{equation*}
    \hat{l}(\theta) = \frac{1}{n_1} \sum_{i=1}^{n_1} \sum_{t=1}^{|o_{i}|} \left[\pi_{\theta}\left(o_{i,t} \mid p', o_{i,<t}\right)T(\hat{A_{i}})\right]
    \end{equation*}
    \vspace{-1em}

    \State Combine $l$, $l'$, $\hat{l}$ and $\mathbb{D}_{\mathrm{KL}}\left[\pi_{\theta} \| \pi_{\mathrm{ref}}\right]$ to form the updated objective function $\mathcal{J}(\theta)$:
    \begin{equation*}
    \mathcal{J}(\theta) = l(\theta) + l'(\theta) + \hat{l}(\theta)-\beta \cdot \mathbb{D}_{\mathrm{KL}}\left[\pi_{\theta} \| \pi_{\mathrm{ref}}\right]
    \end{equation*}
    \State $\theta \leftarrow \theta + \nabla_{\theta} \mathcal{J}(\theta)$ \Comment{Update current policy parameters}
    \State $\theta_{\text{old}} \leftarrow \theta$ \Comment{Update old policy parameters}
\EndFor
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm*}




\subsection{Knowledge capability exploration}

When input with $p'$, model’s reasoning capacity could be assessed across three dimensions: parametric knowledge based reasoning capacity, contextual knowledge based reasoning ability, and reasoning capability under inconsistencies between parametric and contextual knowledge. If the model exhibits competence in all three aspects during reasoning, it can handle contextual information more robustly. We thus aim to identify and optimize the distributions corresponding to these three capabilities within the model. We define the distribution of models representing these three capabilities as ${\pi},{\pi'},{\hat{\pi}}$.

Basicly, we can employ reinforcement learning methods like GRPO to optimize the policy function by sampling $p'$ . This method's unilateral sampling only enhances the ability of ${\pi'} $. Therefore, we augment GRPO with joint sampling of both contextual knowledge and parametric knowledge to improve another two ability.

We sample $n_1$ parametric knowledge reasoning paths $\mathcal{O}=\{o_i\}_{i=1}^{n_1}$ from $p$ and $n_2$ contextual knowledge reasoning paths  $\mathcal{O'}=\{o'_j\}_{j=1}^{n_2}$ from $p'$. 

We then gain  the ${\pi}$ and ${\pi'}$ as following:\\
\vspace{-1em}
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
\pi &= {\pi_{\theta}\left(o_{i, t} \mid p, o_{i,<t}\right)},\\
\pi' &= {\pi_{\theta}\left(o'_{j, t} \mid p', o'_{j,<t}\right)}
\end{split}
\end{equation}
\vspace{-1em}

Now, we need to obtain ${\hat{\pi}}$.
When the model receives  $p'$, its output distribution is ${\pi'}$. We now aim to modify it with partial capabilities of  ${\pi}$, i.e., the ability to reason based on its parametric knowledge under $p'$. To achieve this, we concatenate the parametric knowledge reasoning paths  $\mathcal{O}=\{o_i\}_{i=1}^{n_1}$ with retrieval-augmented prompt $ p'$. This process effectively simulates the distribution of parametric knowledge reasoning when processing  $p' $ thereby obtain ${\hat{\pi}}$, as illustrated below.
\begin{equation}
\hat{\pi} ={\pi_{\theta}\left(o_{i, t} \mid p', o_{i,<t}\right)}\\
\end{equation}

\subsection{Knowledge capability optimization}
We have obtained the distributions corresponding to the three knowledge capabilities. To enhance these capabilities, we need to train our model to maximize the rewards of the three distributions. This approach allows us to dynamically integrate their strengths based on factual correctness, ultimately achieving optimal comprehensive capabilities across all distributions.

Following GRPO, we compute group relativethe advantage for reasoning paths of three distributions . For ${\pi}$ and ${\pi'}$, we derive advantage scores $\mathcal{A}=\{A_i\}_{i=1}^{n_1}$ and $\mathcal{A'}=\{A'_j\}_{j=1}^{n_2}$ that quantify each path's relative quality within their respective groups. we calculate the advantage $A_i$ and $A'_j$  by normalizing their respective group-level rewards $\{R_i\}_{i=1}^{n_1}$ and $\{R'_j\}_{j=1}^{n_2}$ as follows:


\begin{equation}
\scalebox{0.85}{$\displaystyle
A_{i} = \frac{R_{i} - \mean(\{R_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n_1})}{\std(\{R_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n_1})},
$}
\scalebox{0.85}{$\displaystyle
A'_{j} = \frac{R'_{j} - \mean(\{R'_{j}\}_{j=1}^{n_2})}{\std(\{R'_{j}\}_{j=1}^{n_2})}
$}
\end{equation}


% \begin{equation}

% \scalebox{0.85}{
% $\displaystyle
% A_{i} = \frac{R_{i} - \operatorname{mean}(\{R_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n_1})}{\operatorname{std}(\{R_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n_1})},\\
% A'_{j} = \frac{R_{j} - \operatorname{mean}(\{R'_{j}\}_{j=1}^{n_2})}{\operatorname{std}(\{R'_{j}\}_{j=1}^{n_2})}
% $}

% % \end{aligned}
% \end{equation}

This calculation evaluates response quality within two distributions to enhance model capabilities under each. For ${\hat{\pi}}$, we compute its advantages $\mathcal{\hat{A}} =\{\hat{A}_i\}_{i=1}^{n_1}$ by normalizing rewards over $\mathcal{O}\cup\mathcal{O'}$ generated by $p$ and $p'$, rather than using group normalization solely based on $\mathcal{O}$.
This quantifies the relative effectiveness of parametric knowledge versus external knowledge when processing $p' $, ensuring balanced synergy. The advantage of ${\hat{\pi}}$ is calculated as follows:
\begin{equation}
\scalebox{0.85}{$\displaystyle
\hat{A_i}= \frac{R_{i} - \operatorname{mean}(\{R_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n_1} \cup \{R'_{j}\}_{j=1}^{n_2})}{\operatorname{std}(\{R_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n_1} \cup \{R'_{j}\}_{j=1}^{n_2})}$}
\end{equation}


We then caculate policy object $l(\theta) $ , $ l'(\theta)$ , $ \hat{l}(\theta)$ for three distributions as follows:




\begin{equation}
\scalebox{0.85}{$\displaystyle      % 统一缩放比例（0.85 表示缩小到 85%）
\begin{aligned}
&l(\theta) = \frac{1}{n_1} \sum_{\mathclap{i=1}}^{\mathclap{n_1}} \sum_{\mathclap{t=1}}^{\mathclap{|o_i|}} 
    \min \Bigl[ r_{i,t}(\theta) A_i,\; \mathcal{CLIP}\bigl(r_{i,t}(\theta) A_i\bigr) \Bigr], \\
&l'(\theta) = \frac{1}{n_2} \sum_{\mathclap{j=1}}^{\mathclap{n_2}} \sum_{\mathclap{t=1}}^{\mathclap{|o'_j|}} 
    \min \Bigl[ r'_{j,t}(\theta) A'_j,\; \mathcal{CLIP}\bigl(r'_{j,t}(\theta) A'_j\bigr) \Bigr], \\
&\hat{l}(\theta) = \frac{1}{n_1} \sum_{\mathclap{i=1}}^{\mathclap{n_1}} \sum_{\mathclap{t=1}}^{\mathclap{|o_i|}} 
    \Bigl[ \hat{r}_{i,t}(\theta) T(\hat{A}_i) \Bigr], \\
&\text{where} \quad 
    r_{i,t}(\theta) = \frac{\pi_{\theta}(o_{i,t} \mid p, o_{i,<t})}{\pi_{\theta_{\mathrm{old}}}(o_{i,t} \mid p, o_{i,<t})}, \\
&\phantom{\text{where}} \quad 
    r'_{j,t}(\theta) = \frac{\pi_{\theta}(o'_{j,t} \mid p', o'_{j,<t})}{\pi_{\theta_{\mathrm{old}}}(o'_{j,t} \mid p', o'_{j,<t})}, \\
&\phantom{\text{where}} \quad 
    \hat{r}_{i,t}(\theta) = \pi_{\theta}(o_{i,t} \mid p', o_{i,<t}), \\
&\phantom{\text{where}} \quad 
    \mathcal{CLIP}(x) = \operatorname{clip}(x; 1-\epsilon, 1+\epsilon)
\end{aligned}$}
\end{equation}
For the optimization objective of  $\hat{l}(\theta)$, given that our training samples are concatenated and thus lack a reasonable old distribution, we have removed importance sampling. Additionally, to facilitate better exploration of parametric knowledge by the model, we also omit gradient clipping. Besides, we use an advantage function transformation $T$ to $\hat{A_i}$ which will be introduced in \ref{sec:adjustment}.
Our ultimate optimization goal is to maximize the expected rewards of the three distributions under the constraints of $ \mathbb{D}_{\mathrm{KL}}\left[\pi_{\theta} \| \pi_{\mathrm{ref}}\right]$:
  \begin{equation}
    \mathcal{J}(\theta) = l(\theta) + l'(\theta) + \hat{l}(\theta)-\beta \cdot \mathbb{D}_{\mathrm{KL}}\left[\pi_{\theta} \| \pi_{\mathrm{ref}}\right]
    \end{equation}




