% Parametric knowledge in LLMs. After pre-training, Large Language Models (LLMs) have internalized a vast amount of knowledge as parameters, known as parametric knowledge \cite{inui2019proceedings,hu2023survey,gueta2023knowledge}. Despite LLMs' ability to remember a wealth of information, it does not guarantee their proficient retrieval and manipulation of this knowledge for subsequent tasks \cite{berglund2023reversal,liu2023we,wang2024fake,allen2023physics}. Some studies have also observed that incorporating too much or redundant contextual knowledge may lead to catastrophic forgetting of parametric knowledge \cite{wen2024mindmap,wang2025bring}. When contextual information is available, LLMs tend to suppress their parametric knowledge, even if it is complementary or irrelevant. Although tailored prompting can encourage reliance on parametric knowledge, LLMs still struggle to utilize it correctly \cite{cheng2024understandinginterplayparametriccontextual}. Additionally, some works \cite{jeong2024adaptive,fan2024survey} attempt to fuse parametric and contextual knowledge by first generating partial answers using parametric knowledge, followed by integrating the generated knowledge with contextual information to produce the final answer. However, this reasoning process is time-consuming and risks introducing inaccuracies in the estimated parametric knowledge, which may not be detected and corrected.

