The found counterexample contains the system settings, e.g. $n=193$ and $t=64$, an initial configuration as well as a sequence of actions. It reports that the resulted state does not satisfy the formula of  \textit{(CB2)}. We analyze its execution and find that it follows the pattern of the designed attack in~\cite{ABABug:miller:18}: firstly some correct processes access the common coin, then the adaptive adversary obtains the coin value $v$ and manipulates the messages, and finally a correct process gets its new \emph{est} value as $1-v$. By repeating the last actions we can complete the duality in the attack.
%, which can be seen as a concrete attack by an adaptive round-rigid adversary. 
