% ABSTRACT: version 1 (Ben)

The Fréchet distance is a computational mainstay for comparing polygonal curves.
By aligning the curves through translations before computing their distance, one obtains the Fréchet distance under translation, a natural modification that allows the comparison of the movement patterns across different reference frames.
Numerous variants of this problem have been studied, with most work concentrating on discrete distance measures, pointing to a significant gap between the discrete and continuous versions of the Fréchet distance.  

%For curves of complexity $n$ in the plane, the fastest known algorithm for the continuous Fréchet distance under translation runs in $\tilde{\mathcal{O}}(n^8)$ time. In contrast, the discrete Fréchet distance under translation in the plane can be solved in $\tilde{\mathcal{O}}(n^{14/3})$ time. For 1D curves, there is a $\tilde{\mathcal{O}}(n^3)$ time algorithm for the discrete variant~\cite{}.

The problem we tackle is the Fréchet distance under translations for 1D curves, which are fundamental objects that naturally appear in various applications. 
We establish an algorithm for the continuous problem with a running time of $\mathcal{O}(n^{8/3} \log^3 n)$ for curves of complexity $n$, improving the previous state-of-the-art of $\mathcal{O}(n^5\log n)$ and matching the running time of the discrete case.
