\section{Measuring Common Sense in Humans and Statements}
\label{si:sec:commonsense_recap}

Here we describe the commonsensicality framework by Whiting and Watts~\citepSI{whitingFrameworkQuantifyingIndividual2024}, whose data we adopt to study common sense in LLMs. Suppose we have a set of $n_s \geq 1$ statements (see \Cref{si:tab:statement_sources} for an example) and a set of $n_p \geq 1$ people. (In this case, $n_s = 4,407$ and $n_p = 2,046$.) Given a statement, such as ``If you want to play a \emph{[sic]} guitar then you should take lessons,'' a human participant was asked two questions:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item[(a)] ``Do you agree with this statement?''
    \item[(b)] ``Do you think most people would agree with this statement?''
\end{enumerate}

Common sense is measured for both humans and statements. The underlying rationale is that a person with common sense knows what is true (in the sense that their belief coincides with the general human consensus), and also knows what other people believe to be true (in the sense that they can accurately predict what the majority of other people believe). Similarly, a statement is common sense if it is (nearly) unanimously agreed upon by people in this population, who must also accurately predict what the human consensus on this statement is. All of this depends on participants' answers to the two questions above.

To make this precise, define $A$ be the $n_s \times n_p$ matrix such that 
\begin{align}
    A_{i, j} = \begin{cases}
      1 & \text{if the $j$-th participant answered ``yes'' to question (a) about the $i$-th claim} \\
      0 & \text{if the $j$-th participant answered ``no'' to question (a) about the $i$-th claim}.
    \end{cases}
    \label{si:eq:human_ratings_q1}
\end{align}
In other words, the matrix $A$ contains participants' answers to question (a) above. Similarly, let $B$ be the $n_s \times n_p$ matrix containing answers to question (b) such that
\begin{align}
    B_{i, j} = \begin{cases}
      1 & \text{if the $j$-th participant answered ``yes'' to question (b) about the $i$-th claim} \\
      0 & \text{if the $j$-th participant answered ``no'' to question (b) about the $i$-th claim}.
    \end{cases}
    \label{si:eq:human_ratings_q2}
\end{align}

One complication in this study is that not every claim was labeled by everyone, because every participant was required to label only a small number of claims, 50 to be exact. Every $(i, j)$ entry in matrices $A$ and $B$, therefore, is only valid if the $i$-th claim was indeed assigned to the $i$-th participant; otherwise, participant $j$ would have never seen and labeled claim $i$ in the first place, and hence the entry must not be taken into account. Hence, $A$ and $B$ are partially filled matrices.

Define $\Omega_i \subseteq \{ 1, 2, \ldots, n_p \}$ to be the set of participants who were indeed assigned to rate the $i$-th statement, and $\Phi_j \subseteq \{ 1, 2, \ldots, n_s \}$ to be the set of statements that were indeed assigned to the $j$-th participant. We hence have $| \Phi_j | = 50$, where $| \cdot |$ denotes the size of a finite set.

Before we go on to define commonsensicality, define the \emph{human rating distribution} for each statement $i$ as the proportion of people that indicated they agreed with statement $i$ (\ie, by answering ``yes'' to question (a)):
\begin{align}
    d_{i}^{h, a} = \frac{1}{|\Omega_i|} \sum_{j \in \Omega_i} A_{i, j}.
    \label{si:eq:human_ratings_dist}
\end{align}

