\subsection{Statement-Level Commonsensicality}
\label{si:sec:commonsense_recap:statement_commonsensicality}

The commonsensicality of a statement is a combination of its two scores, consensus and awareness.

Given a statement $i$, its \emph{consensus} score measures how close people's judgments on this statement are to absolute unanimity.
\begin{align}
    c_i^h = 2 \times \left| d_{i}^{h,a} - 0.5 \right|.
    \label{si:eq:statement_consensus}
\end{align}
High consensus requires that the human rating distribution $d_{i}^{h,a}$ to be very close to 0 (everyone disagrees with $i$) or 1 (everyone agrees with $i$). Note that this definition is symmetric with respect to the polarity of opinions. For example, a statement which was agreed by 60\% of participants would have the same consensus score as a statement which was disagreed by 60\% of participants.

In addition, given statement $i$, its \emph{awareness} score measures how accurately people perceived its majority rating. Formally, we have
\begin{align}
    a_i^h = \frac{1}{|\Omega_i|} \sum_{j \in \Omega_i} \mathbbm{1} \left[ B_{i, j} = \texttt{majority}_i^h \right].
    \label{si:eq:statement_awareness}
\end{align}
High awareness requires that people know what most others believe, which is recorded in question (b) above.

For a statement to be common sense, it must score high in both consensus and awareness. The \emph{commonsensicality} score of statement $i$ is defined as the geometric mean of its consensus and awareness scores:
\begin{align}
    m_i^h = \sqrt{c_i^h \times a_i^h}.
    \label{si:eq:statement_commonsensicality}
\end{align}
The commonsensicality score $m_i^h$ is also between 0 and 1 and values closer to 1 indicate higher degrees of common sense.

\Cref{si:fig:commonsensicality} (bottom) on page \pageref{si:fig:commonsensicality} presents statements on two dimensions: consensus and awareness. Generally, statements received a higher awareness scores than consensus scores. The figure also shows that most statements received a high commonsensicality score, with a median of 70.12\%.

\subsection{Human-Level Commonsensicality}
\label{si:sec:commonsense_recap:human_commonsensicality}

Commonsensicality is defined also for people, comprising consensus and awareness scores.

Given a person $j$, their \emph{consensus} score is defined as the fraction of times they agreed with the majority rating across all statements they rated:
\begin{align}
    C_j = \frac{1}{|\Phi_j|} \sum_{i \in \Phi_j} \mathbbm{1} \left[ A_{i, j} = \texttt{majority}_i^h \right].
    \label{si:eq:human_consensus}
\end{align}
To have high consensus, person $j$'s own ratings of the statements, via their answer to question (a) above, must coincide with the overall human majority ratings.

The \emph{awareness} score of person $j$ is the fraction of time they accurately predicted the majority rating across all statements:
\begin{align}
    A_j = \frac{1}{|\Phi_j|} \sum_{i \in \Phi_j} \mathbbm{1} \left[ B_{i, j} = \texttt{majority}_i^h \right].
    \label{si:eq:human_awareness}
\end{align}
To have a high awareness score, person $j$'s prediction of what most other people think---via question (b) above, and regardless of what they chose for question (a)---must also coincide with the overall human majority ratings.

Consensus $C_j$ and awareness $A_j$ scores are also between 0 and 1. Similarly, we take the geometric mean of these scores to give participant $j$'s \emph{commonsensicality} score as 
\begin{align}
    M_j = \sqrt{C_j \times A_j}.
    \label{si:eq:human_commonsensicality}
\end{align}
This score is also between 0 and 1 with higher values indicating higher degrees of common sense in a person.

\Cref{si:fig:commonsensicality} (top) on page \pageref{si:fig:commonsensicality} also presents humans on two dimensions: consensus and awareness. Different from statements, humans tended to exhibit very similar scores on both dimensions (most of them lie close to the diagonal line). The median commonsensicality for humans is 79.43\%.


