\section{Collecting Responses from Language Models}
\label{si:sec:lm_prompt}

To assess common sense in LLMs, we select a number of models that have been trained to follow conversations (instruction-tuned models). The full list of models we experiment with is found in \Cref{si:tab:model_list} on page \pageref{si:tab:model_list}. A typical conversation with an LLM is as follows.
\begin{itemize}
    \item[] \texttt{[SYSTEM PROMPT]}
    \item[] \texttt{[USER PROMPT]}
    \item[] \texttt{[ASSISTANT RESPONSE]}
    \item[] \texttt{[USER PROMPT]}
    \item[] \texttt{[ASSISTANT RESPONSE]}
    \item[] \texttt{\ldots}
\end{itemize}
The conversation starts with an optional \emph{system prompt}, which is used to steer the general behavior of the LLM. For example, one can set this to ``You are a debate expert'' if the model is intended to be used to make arguments. Then, the system prompt is followed by a series of alternating \emph{user prompts} and \emph{assistant responses}. All messages in this conversation are concatenated into a \emph{chat history}, which is a string of the form \texttt{[SYSTEM PROMPT] [SEP] [USER PROMPT] [SEP] [ASSISTANT RESPONSE] [SEP] [USER PROMPT] [SEP] \newline [ASSISTANT RESPONSE]\ldots} The \texttt{[SEP]} symbol is a special token denoting the separation of messages, and is specific to each model.

To use an LLM to generate an assistant response, one prepares a chat history that ends with a user prompt, and hence expects an assistant response to follow from it. The model takes the chat history as input and autoregressively generates new tokens one by one, until a finishing condition is reached. These tokens are then appended to the chat history, thereby completing the assistant response. Instruction-tuned models are specifically trained in this manner, via many such conversations, and hence we choose these models for assessment.

\Cref{si:code:lm_probs} shows a concrete example of our procedure, which is described in more detail in the below subsections.

\subsection{Designing Prompts and Expected Responses}
\label{si:sec:lm_prompt:prompts}

We decide not to use any specific system prompt. For some models, this is set to their default system prompts, such as ``You are a helpful assistant.'' For some other models, this can be left blank, so the chat history actually starts with a user prompt.

For each of the $n_s = 4,407$ statements, we prompt a model to respond to the following two use prompts:
\begin{itemize}
    \item[(a)] \texttt{Consider the following statement, "[STATEMENT]" Do you agree with\\ this statement? Start your answer with a "yes" or "no".}
    \item[(b)] \texttt{Consider the following statement, "[STATEMENT]" Do you  think most\\ people would agree with this statement? Start your answer with a \\"yes" or "no".}
\end{itemize}

These are the same as questions (a) and (b) described in \Cref{si:sec:commonsense_recap}. We note three details of these prompts. First, questions (a) and (b) are used in two separate chat sessions. This is because any chat history can modify a model's answer in unexpected ways, which we do not like to experience. Second, we include the sentence ``Start your answer with a `yes' or `no'.'' at the end of each prompt to instruct the model to output a ``yes'' or ``no'' directly. Without this explicit instruction, the model may start its response with, for example, ``I agree with the answer,'' or give some reasoning before giving its final answer. Either way, extracting the binary answer from would be more complicated and noisy. And third, in framing the questions in this way, we need the model to output only one token---``yes'' or ``no''---and we can extract the probability with which it generates either token, which represents the model's inherent uncertainty in its answer (more below).

\clearpage
\begin{lstlisting}[
    language=Python,
    caption={\small Python code used to extract an open-source model's answer to an example prompt. Here, we demonstrate this process using the model DBRX. Note that there could be multiple tokens within this model's vocabulary that can be mapped to the answers ``yes'' and ``no,'' hence we need to enumerate them in the \texttt{answer2tokid} dictionary on lines 14--17. Based on the prompt (lines 19--22), the model is supposed to start its response with a definitive ``yes'' or ``no''. Therefore, we perform one feedforward operation to get the probabilities of all tokens that can be generated next (lines 29--33). We then sum the probabilities of all tokens that map to ``yes'' and ``no'' to get the model's probability of answering with either option (lines 36--43).},
    captionpos=b,
    label={si:code:lm_probs}
]
# Load tokenizer and model
tokenizer = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(MODEL_NAME)
model = model = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(
    "databricks/dbrx-instruct",
    device_map="auto",
    trust_remote_code=True,
    torch_dtype=torch.bfloat16,
)
model.eval(); model.tie_weights()

# These are all the IDs of the tokens in DBRX's vocabulary that map to 
# the answers "yes" and "no".
answer2tokid = {
    "yes": [9642, 60844, 14331, 58841, 20137, 60665, 7566, 85502, 77830, 95934, 10035, 86508, 98171, 41898, 14410, 9891], 
    "no": [72719, 6673, 42257, 86176, 43983, 67579, 2822, 79027, 18847, 40305, 34200, 58749, 5782, 38089, 39522, 73204, 9173, 99076, 2360, 38557, 31415, 12674, 85298, 29466, 2201, 912, 61559, 17184, 51899, 9278]
}

# Formulate the prompt
messages = [
    {"role": "user",
     "content": 'Consider the statement, "A desire to share knowledge would make you want to teach other people." Do you agree with this statement? Start your answer with a "yes" or "no".'}
]

# Encode the message
inputs = tokenizer.apply_chat_template(messages, add_generation_prompt=True)

# Feedforward the input
inputs = torch.tensor(inputs, device=model.device, dtype=torch.long).reshape(1, -1)
outputs = model(inputs, output_hidden_states=False)

# Extract the probability of immediate next token
probs = torch.softmax(outputs[0][0, -1], axis=0)
probs = probs.cpu().detach().float().numpy()

# Map token IDs to answers
answer_probs = {}
# The probability that the model answers "yes" is the sum of the
# probabilities of all tokens that map to "yes".
answer_probs["yes"] = float(probs[answer2tokid["yes"]].sum())
# Similar for "no".
answer_probs["no"] = float(probs[answer2tokid["no"]].sum())
# The probability that the model answers with something else.
answer_probs["other"] = 1 - answer_probs["yes"] - answer_probs["no"]
\end{lstlisting}

