\subsection{Extracting Responses from LLMs}
\label{si:sec:lm_prompt:responses}

Questions (a) and (b) in \Cref{si:sec:lm_prompt:prompts} are formulated as user prompts for each model, which is then expected to generate a response starting with a ``yes'' or ``no.'' As explained above, the model does so by autoregression: it maintains a multinomial probability distribution over all possible tokens and randomly chooses a token to generate via sampling from this distribution. In other words, the tokens ``yes'' and ``no'' are each assigned a probability with which they are generated. This also means that every other token may be generated, as long as its probability is not zero, leading to ``illegal'' answers. We address this problem in the following.

First, a review of how we can obtain tokens' probabilities for each model.
\begin{itemize}
    \item For GPT-3.5 and GPT-4, OpenAI's API allows us to have access to up to 20 tokens associated with the highest sampling probabilities via the \texttt{logprobs} parameter.
    \item For Gemini 1.0 Pro, Claude 3 and Mistral-Large, their APIs do not allow access to these probabilities. We repeatedly ask the same question multiple times and report the empirical frequencies of the generated tokens, which give us an unbiased estimate of the probabilities. We perform 23 repetitions for Claude 3 and Mistral-Large, which matches the average number of people that rated each question in the original dataset \citepSI{whitingFrameworkQuantifyingIndividual2024}. For Gemini 1.0 Pro, we perform 50 repetitions.
    \item For all other models, which are open-weight and loaded via the Hugging Face library in Python, we have access to the probabilities of all tokens.
\end{itemize}

In generating tokens, we set all sampling parameters to default; most notably, we keep the sampling temperature at 1.0. Once we have the probabilities of generated tokens, we perform the following processing steps.
\begin{itemize}
    \item We discard all tokens that do not represent ``yes'' or ``no'' answers. This usually happens when a model wants to avoid giving an answer, for example, ``As an AI model I cannot\ldots'' which leads to the first generated token being ``As,'' an invalid token.
    \item For some models, their tokenizers have different variants of the same answer, due to formatting reasons. For example, the answer ``yes'' can be generated from the tokens \texttt{Yes}, \texttt{yes}, \verb*| yes|, \texttt{"Yes}, \etcetera, which should all be valid. In this case, we compile a list of all such valid tokens and sum over their probabilities to get the final score.
    \item After this, we have probabilities for the ``yes'' and ``no'' answers. Since we have discarded all illegal tokens, these two probabilities do not necessarily add up to one. We hence rescale them by dividing each by their sum. See \Cref{si:code:lm_probs}.
\end{itemize}
The resulting binomial distribution of answers is called the \emph{model rating distribution} and denoted
\begin{align}
    d_{i}^{m, a} = p(\text{``yes''} \mid \text{prompt (a) for statement } i).
    \label{si:eq:model_ratings_dist}
\end{align}
where $i$ denotes a statement and $m$ denotes a model ($m \in \{1, \ldots, n_m\}$, $n_m$ being the number of models). Compare this with the human rating distribution in \Cref{si:eq:human_ratings_dist} on page \pageref{si:eq:human_ratings_dist}.

Similarly, the probability that the model thinks most people would agree with statement $i$ is 
\begin{align}
    d_{i}^{m, b} = p(\text{``yes''} \mid \text{prompt (b) for statement } i).
    \label{si:eq:model_ratings_dist_q2}
\end{align}

\Cref{si:fig:probs_q1_freqs_humans_models} (top) on page \pageref{si:fig:probs_q1_freqs_humans_models} shows the frequencies of the human and model rating distributions. As depicted, human judgments are widely varied with many statements receiving close to 50/50 ratings. For most models, on the other hand, these probabilities are predominantly driven to either 0 and 1. The same is observed for humans' and models' answers to question (b).

