\section{Measuring Individual-Level Commonsensicality in LLMs}
\label{si:sec:model_commonsensicality}

In this section, we consider each language model as a hypothetical human survey respondent, just like those described in \Cref{si:sec:commonsense_recap:human_commonsensicality}.

Start from $d_{i}^{m, a}$ (\Cref{si:eq:model_ratings_dist}), which is the probability that the model agrees with statement $i$, via its answer to question (a). We binarize this probabilistic output by taking its mode
\begin{align}
    \alpha_i^{m} = \mathbbm{1}[d_{i}^{m, a} \geq 0.5].
\end{align}
In other words, we consider that the model agrees with statement $i$ (\ie, $\alpha_i^{m, a} = 1$) if and only if it agrees with this statement with a probability of at least 0.5.

Similarly, the model believes that most people would agree with this statement if and only if it answers ``yes'' to question (b) with a probability of at least 0.5:
\begin{align}
    \beta_i^{m} = \mathbbm{1}[d_{i}^{m, b} \geq 0.5].
\end{align}

Based on these two binary answers, the model's \emph{consensus} and \emph{awareness} scores, respectively, are
\begin{align}
    C_m &= \frac{1}{n_s} \sum_{i=1}^{n_s} \mathbbm{1} \left[ \alpha_i^m = \texttt{majority}_i^h \right], \label{si:eq:model_consensus} \\
    A_m &= \frac{1}{n_s} \sum_{i=1}^{n_s} \mathbbm{1} \left[ \beta_i^m = \texttt{majority}_i^h \right],\label{si:eq:model_awareness}
\end{align}
where $\texttt{majority}_i^h$ is the human majority rating for statement $i$ (\Cref{si:eq:human_majority_rating}). Compare these calculations with the consensus and awareness scores for humans, in \Cref{si:eq:human_consensus,si:eq:human_awareness}.

Finally, similar to \Cref{si:eq:human_commonsensicality}, the model's \emph{commonsensicality} score is the geometric mean of its consensus and awareness scores: 
\begin{align}
    M_m = \sqrt{C_m \times A_m}.
    \label{si:eq:model_commonsensicality}
\end{align}
All three scores are between 0 and 1, and higher values indicate closer proximity to absolute common sense. The goal of this calculation is to compare $M_m$ with $M_j$, the commonsensicality of person $j$ defined in \Cref{si:eq:human_commonsensicality}.

\Cref{si:tab:model_individual_commonsensicality} shows every model's consesus, awareness and Commonsensicality scores.

\subsection{Model Size and Commonsensicality}
\label{si:sec:model_commonsensicality:effect_of_model_size}
To investigate the relationship between a model's size and its commonsensicality score, we select from \Cref{si:tab:model_list} (on page \pageref{si:tab:model_list}) 6 model families: Falcon, Flan-T5, Gemma, LLaMA-2/3, Mistral and Qwen2. Our selection criterion is that the family must include at least 2 models for which we know the sizes. See \Cref{si:tab:model_individual_commonsensicality}. We also exclude Gemini 1.0 Pro in the Gemma/Gemini family and Mistral-Large in the Mistral family because these models are proprietary with unknown sizes. For GPT, although we know that GPT-3.5 has 175 billion parameters, we do not know this information for GPT-4.

These 6 families give us 23 models in total. Since model commonsensicality varies substantially across families, we perform a mixed-effects regression analysis to analyze the relationship between model size and commonsensicality. In R's syntax, the model is described by the following formula:
\begin{align}
    \texttt{commonsensicality} ~\sim~ \log_{10}(\texttt{model size}) + (1 \mid \texttt{model family}).
    \label{si:eq:model_commonsensicality_vs_size_mixed_eff}
\end{align}
We also log-transform model size since these models can be orders of magnitude different from each other. In addition, we establish the 95\% confidence interval of the effect of (log) model size using the \texttt{confint} function in R.

\subsection{Model General Performance and Commonsensicality}
\label{si:sec:model_commonsensicality:effect_of_model_elo}

There are numerous general benchmarks of LLMs available publicly. We choose the LMSYS Chatbot Arena benchmark \citepSI{chiangChatbotArenaOpen2024} to represent a model's general performance, relative to other models.

LMSYS compares 114 LLMs models side by side, which include 23 models in our collection. When a user visits their website,\footnote{\url{https://chat.lmsys.org}} two models are randomly chosen. The user then interacts with these models simultaneously, asking the same question and getting corresponding answers from both. Then the user decides whether they prefer the left or the right model. (They also can choose to tie them, or to indicate both models are bad.)

Models in this collection are then assigned an Elo score, with higher values indicating a higher chance of being preferred by humans. Essentially, the Elo scores are used to approximate the win-rate matrix, and these scores are estimated by Chiang \etal~\citepSI{chiangChatbotArenaOpen2024} using the Bradley-Terry maximum likelihood method.

Refer to the original paper \citepSI{chiangChatbotArenaOpen2024} for the exact calculation. The Elo score by LMSYS can be interpreted as follows. Suppose two models, $a$ and $b$, receives their respective Elo scores of $R_a$ and $R_b$. Then the probability that model $a$ is chosen over model $b$ is
\begin{align}
    \frac{1}{1 + 10^{\frac{R_b - R_a}{400}}}.
    \label{si:eq:elo_interp}
\end{align}
For example, GPT-4o and DBRX have scores of 1,287 and 1,103, respectively. The probability that DBRX is preferred over GPT-4o is
\begin{align*}
    \frac{1}{1 + 10^{\frac{1,287 - 1,103}{400}}} \approx 0.257.
\end{align*}
In other words, GPT-4o is expected to win about $100\% - 25.7\% = 74.3\%$ of the time. Elo scores are invariant to translation, \ie, if all scores shift up or down by the same amount, the interpretation above does not change. In this paper, we keep the original scores that are reported on LMSYS Arena.

Since this calculation is updated over time on LMSYS Arena---as more models are added to the benchmark and more users participate in evaluation---we report the scores that are the most updated at the time of writing, July 8, 2024. Models' Elo scores are calculated based on 1,530,830 pairwise comparisons.

Finally, for the 23 models in our collection that also exist on LMSYS Arena (see \Cref{si:tab:model_individual_commonsensicality}), we also extract their commonsensicality scores and evaluate the Pearson correlation between the two types of scores.

\subsection{Comparing Models and Humans Side by Side on Commonsensicality}
\label{si:sec:model_commonsensicality:pair_comparison}

When comparing a model and a human directly on their commonsensicality scores, note that each person was only tasked with labeling 50 randomly chosen statements, whereas a model labels all 4,407 of them. For a fair comparison, we restrict the calculation of consensus, awareness and commonsensicality of the model only to the 50 statements that the person labeled.

Specifically, for person $j$ the calculations in \Cref{si:eq:human_consensus,si:eq:human_awareness,si:eq:human_commonsensicality} do not change because they are already with respect to $\Phi_j$, the subset of statements that were labeled by $j$. To compare model $m$ with person $j$, we modify \Cref{si:eq:model_consensus,si:eq:model_awareness} above so that the average is with respect to $\Phi_j$:
\begin{align*}
    C_m &= \frac{1}{|\Phi_j|} \sum_{i \in \Phi_j} \mathbbm{1} \left[ \alpha_i^m = \texttt{majority}_i^h \right], \\
    A_m &= \frac{1}{|\Phi_j|} \sum_{i \in \Phi_j} \mathbbm{1} \left[ \beta_i^m = \texttt{majority}_i^h \right].
\end{align*}

