	\subsubsection{Eigenvalues thresholding\label{sec03c}}	
	As mentioned earlier, the SVD algorithm orders these eigenvectors in descending order of their respective singular values. Thus, it is possible to draw an analogy between the sum of the singular values and the energy corresponding to the sum of the components of a given signal. The thresholding we apply is quite simple: we retain all singular values (and their corresponding singular vectors) whose cumulative sum exceeds a predefined threshold. As an illustration, Figure \ref{fig:03} shows the first 100 singular values (\cf $\Sigma$ ) obtained after diagonalizing the matrix \textbf{H}, constructed from the first component of the time series of the rotational polar motion. We will revisit this time series in the next section. In black are the singular values before thresholding, and in red are the singular values selected after applying a 90\% threshold. In other words, 90\% of the energy of this time series is represented by the 37 red singular values (and their corresponding singular vectors).

