Graph Retrieval-Augmented Generation (GraphRAG), an enhancement technique for LLMs introduced by Microsoft Research \cite{GraphRAG}, enables LLMs to extract entities and their relationships from unstructured text. It constructs a structured knowledge graph and utilizes retrieval mechanisms to enhance its comprehension of entity relationships. In the medical domain, this method has been proposed \cite{KG-LLM-medicine-idea}, and its effectiveness has been demonstrated \cite{KG-LLM-medicine-work1}\cite{KG-LLM-medicine-work2}\cite{KG-LLM-medicine-work3}.In biological research, although existing studies have employed external data to enhance LLMs' biological insights \cite{DrBioRight}, knowledge graph construction has not been utilised to improve the retrieval and comprehension of biological entity information.

A potential limitation contributing to this phenomenon may stem from GraphRAG's requirement for task-specific, high-quality datasets. We attempted to construct a knowledge graph using the raw CellMarker2.0 dataset \cite{cellmarker}, but the LLM still struggled to accurately identify the most probable cell type due to an overwhelming number of candidates. To address the data problem, we reconstructed the CellMarker2.0 database to make it more annotation-oriented. Additionally, we designed a multi-task workflow that simulates the manual annotation process, which systematically improves the annotation quality.


% Inspired by this approach, we designed a knowledge graph system tailored for cell type annotation, simulating the manual annotation process. Specifically, we employed an LLM as a cell annotation expert, enabling it to retrieve structured knowledge from the CellMarker2.0\cite{cellmarker} database. However, in practical applications, a major challenge emerged: due to the large number of cell types associated with marker gene detection, LLMs struggled to capture the complex relationships between cell types and marker genes, leading to reduced annotation accuracy. To address this, we optimised the CellMarker2.0 database to make it more annotation-oriented and designed a multi-agent workflow to systematically improve the annotation.
